# the changes game mutants lets by, one per line as NAME :: WHY, the
# name as the report prints it. a change belongs here only when it
# makes no difference to anything the package promises; a change a
# test could tell apart gets the test instead.
# hud: changes that make no difference to what it draws.
hud/elements.go lay: x <= last.X => x < last.X :: the last column's top and bottom are corners, set after
hud/elements.go lay: y <= last.Y => y < last.Y :: the last row's first and last are corners, set after
hud/picture.go Picture: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: mono reads go's own font, built into the binary; it does not fail
hud/picture.go mono: if err != nil { return nil, fmt.Errorf("%w: %w", ErrHud, err) } => if err != nil { } :: mono reads go's own font, built into the binary; it does not fail
hud/picture.go paint: share >= 0 => share > 0 :: a space draws nothing, as a block of no eighths or as a letter
hud/picture.go paint: share >= 0 => share >= 1 :: a space draws nothing, as a block of no eighths or as a letter
hud/picture.go eighthsOf: return -1 => return -2 :: eighthsOf is asked only whether it is below nought
hud/terminal.go behind: 6 => 7 :: a cell's pattern is its six squares; a seventh bit is never set
hud/view.go Project: return 0, 0, false, err => return 1, 0, false, err :: shares given beside an error are never read
hud/view.go Project: return 0, 0, false, err => return 0, 1, false, err :: shares given beside an error are never read
hud/view.go Project: return 0, 0, false, nil => return 1, 0, false, nil :: shares given for a point not ahead are never read
hud/view.go Project: return 0, 0, false, nil => return 0, 1, false, nil :: shares given for a point not ahead are never read
hud/view.go Hit: hit && t*scale < best => hit && t*scale <= best :: a thing met exactly as far as the best is as near
set/hud.go keysOf: i+1 < len(block.Content) => i+1 <= len(block.Content) :: a block's content is keys and values in pairs


mesh/span.go emptySpan: far := math.Inf(1) => far := math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
mesh/tree.go best: axis, split, cost := 0, 0, math.Inf(1) => axis, split, cost := 0, 0, math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
mesh/tree.go binned: split, cost := 0, math.Inf(1) => split, cost := 0, math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
mesh/tree.go best: if !(middles.high[along] > middles.low[along]) { continue } => if !(middles.high[along] > middles.low[along]) { } :: an axis the middles do not spread on puts every face in bin 0, so binned finds no cut and returns an endless cost, which never beats the best; skipping the axis or not reaches the same answer
mesh/tree.go best: !(middles.high[along] > middles.low[along]) => !(middles.high[along] >= middles.low[along]) :: an axis the middles do not spread on puts every face in bin 0, so binned finds no cut and returns an endless cost, which never beats the best; skipping the axis or not reaches the same answer
mesh/tree.go binned: i > 0 => i >= 0 :: rightAreas[0] and rightCounts[0] are never read: the cuts read the right side from bin 1 up
mesh/near.go Inside: return sum > 2*math.Pi => return sum >= 2*math.Pi :: a point not on the surface has solid angles that come to nought or to whole turns of four pi, one for each closed piece it is inside, so any bound between the two sorts the same; on the surface, where the sum is in between, Inside is not promised and Distance is nought either way
mesh/near.go Inside: return sum > 2*math.Pi => return sum > 2/math.Pi :: a point not on the surface has solid angles that come to nought or to whole turns of four pi, one for each closed piece it is inside, so any bound between the two sorts the same; on the surface, where the sum is in between, Inside is not promised and Distance is nought either way
mesh/near.go Inside: return sum > 2*math.Pi => return sum > 3*math.Pi :: a point not on the surface has solid angles that come to nought or to whole turns of four pi, one for each closed piece it is inside, so any bound between the two sorts the same; on the surface, where the sum is in between, Inside is not promised and Distance is nought either way
mesh/near.go nearest: best := found{face: -1, squared: math.Inf(1)} => best := found{face: +1, squared: math.Inf(1)} :: the first face tried is nearer than endless and replaces it, for any point that is not itself endless; only Normal reads the face, and the package does not cover such a point
mesh/near.go nearest: best := found{face: -1, squared: math.Inf(1)} => best := found{face: -2, squared: math.Inf(1)} :: the first face tried is nearer than endless and replaces it, for any point that is not itself endless; only Normal reads the face, and the package does not cover such a point
mesh/near.go nearest: best := found{face: -1, squared: math.Inf(1)} => best := found{face: -1, squared: math.Inf(2)} :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
mesh/near.go nearest: stack[0] = waiting{node: 0, enter: awayFrom(&made.tree.nodes[0], at)} => stack[1] = waiting{node: 0, enter: awayFrom(&made.tree.nodes[0], at)} :: top starts at 1, so the walk pops stack[0], a node 0 at nought: the root is entered at nought instead of its distance, and only a node past the best is passed over, which the root never is
mesh/near.go closest: d1 <= 0 && d2 <= 0 => d1 < 0 && d2 <= 0 :: at the edge of the corner's region the next region's formula takes the point, with a share of exactly nought along its side, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: d1 <= 0 && d2 <= 0 => d1 <= 0 && d2 < 0 :: at the edge of the corner's region the next region's formula takes the point, with a share of exactly nought along its side, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: d3 >= 0 && d4 <= d3 => d3 > 0 && d4 <= d3 :: at the edge of the corner's region the next region's formula takes the point, with a share of exactly nought or one along its side, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: d3 >= 0 && d4 <= d3 => d3 >= 0 && d4 < d3 :: at the edge of the corner's region the next region's formula takes the point, with a share of exactly nought or one along its side, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: d6 >= 0 && d5 <= d6 => d6 > 0 && d5 <= d6 :: at the edge of the corner's region the next region's formula takes the point, with a share of exactly nought or one along its side, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: d6 >= 0 && d5 <= d6 => d6 >= 0 && d5 < d6 :: at the edge of the corner's region the next region's formula takes the point, with a share of exactly nought or one along its side, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: vc <= 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 <= 0 => vc < 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 <= 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: vc <= 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 <= 0 => vc <= 0 && d1 > 0 && d3 <= 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: vc <= 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 <= 0 => vc <= 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 < 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 => vb < 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 => vb <= 0 && d2 > 0 && d6 <= 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 => vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 < 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 => va < 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 => va <= 0 && d4-d3 > 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/near.go closest: va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 => va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 > 0 :: at the edge of the side's region the neighbour's formula takes the point: on the side's line the face's own weights leave the third at nought, and at either end the share is exactly nought or one, which is the corner
mesh/meet.go package: loosened = 1 + 2*(3*0x1p-53/(1-3*0x1p-53)) => loosened = 1 + 2*(3*0x1p-53*(1-3*0x1p-53)) :: each rounds to the same double as the real, one and three steps of 2^-52, which the slack test holds; the changes are far below a step
mesh/meet.go package: loosened = 1 + 2*(3*0x1p-53/(1-3*0x1p-53)) => loosened = 1 + 2*(3*0x1p-53/(1+3*0x1p-53)) :: each rounds to the same double as the real, one and three steps of 2^-52, which the slack test holds; the changes are far below a step
mesh/meet.go package: loosened = 1 + 2*(3*0x1p-53/(1-3*0x1p-53)) => loosened = 1 + 2*(3*0x1p-53/(1-4*0x1p-53)) :: each rounds to the same double as the real, one and three steps of 2^-52, which the slack test holds; the changes are far below a step
mesh/meet.go aimed: look.inverse[axis] = math.Inf(1) => look.inverse[axis] = math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
mesh/meet.go enters: return enter, enter <= leave*loosened && !math.IsInf(enter, 1) => return enter, enter <= leave*loosened && !math.IsInf(enter, 2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
mesh/meet.go aimed: look.inverse[axis] < 0 => look.inverse[axis] <= 0 :: an inverse is nought only for a part endlessly large, which a ray does not have; it is never exactly nought
mesh/meet.go aimed: way[look.kz] < 0 => way[look.kz] <= 0 :: the largest part is nought only for a ray with no way at all, which meets nothing whichever way its other two axes are taken
mesh/meet.go enters: slab > enter => slab >= enter :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number
mesh/meet.go enters: slab < leave => slab <= leave :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number
mesh/meet.go enters: slab > enter => slab >= enter :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number
mesh/meet.go enters: slab < leave => slab <= leave :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number
mesh/meet.go enters: slab > enter => slab >= enter :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number
mesh/meet.go enters: slab < leave => slab <= leave :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number
mesh/meet.go meets: return 0, weights{}, false => return 1, weights{}, false :: a face that is not met has no distance and no weights: the leaf reads them only when it is met
mesh/meet.go meets: return 0, weights{}, false => return 1, weights{}, false :: a face that is not met has no distance and no weights: the leaf reads them only when it is met
mesh/meet.go meets: return 0, weights{}, false => return 1, weights{}, false :: a face that is not met has no distance and no weights: the leaf reads them only when it is met
mesh/meet.go meets: if whole == 0 { return 0, weights{}, false } => if whole == 0 { } :: the shares are of one sign, so they come to nought only when all are nought, and then the distance is nought over nought, which no bound holds; the face is not met either way
mesh/meet.go meets: !(along > near && along < far) => !(along > near && along <= far) :: a face exactly at far is not nearer than the best, which starts at far, and beaten refuses a tie with it: the face is not kept either way
mesh/meet.go Meet: best := Met{along: far, face: -1} => best := Met{along: far, face: -2} :: the face of the best at the start is read only as met or not, below nought, and the tie rule asks for an earlier face than it, of which there is none below nought
mesh/meet.go Meet: stack[0] = waiting{node: 0, enter: enter} => stack[1] = waiting{node: 0, enter: enter} :: top starts at 1, so the walk pops stack[0], a node 0 at nought: the root is entered at nought instead of its distance, and only a node past the best is passed over, which the root never is
mesh/meet.go Meet: next.enter > best.along*loosened => next.enter >= best.along*loosened :: a box entered at or past the slack holds faces only within the slack of the best, and the mesh promises the nearest face within rounding, not to the bit; so entering it or passing it over gives a face as near as the mesh says. no test can reach equality, and none is claimed
mesh/mesh.go units: if normals == nil { return nil } => if normals == nil { } :: cloning no normals is no normals, and the loop over none does nothing: the result is nil either way
mesh/wind.go runners: return make(map[edge]int, 3*faces) => return make(map[edge]int, 3/faces) :: a size given to make is a hint for the first allocation, and a table grows past it; the table holds the same
mesh/wind.go runners: return make(map[edge]int, 3*faces) => return make(map[edge]int, 4*faces) :: a size given to make is a hint for the first allocation, and a table grows past it; the table holds the same
mesh/wind.go piecesOf: which := make(map[int]int, 1) => which := make(map[int]int, 2) :: a size given to make is a hint for the first allocation, and a table grows past it; the table holds the same
mesh/wind.go volume: origin := points[faces[piece[0]][0]] => origin := points[faces[piece[1]][0]] :: the signed volumes of a closed piece come to the same sum from any point, and it is only the sign that is read; a corner of the piece keeps the numbers small
mesh/wind.go volume: origin := points[faces[piece[0]][0]] => origin := points[faces[piece[0]][1]] :: the signed volumes of a closed piece come to the same sum from any point, and it is only the sign that is read; a corner of the piece keeps the numbers small
planet/column.go NewLayer: math.Abs(sum-1) > 1e-9 => math.Abs(sum-1) >= 1e-9 :: differs only when the shares miss one by exactly 1e-9, which no sum of float shares lands on
planet/methane.go methaneRows: vacuum, err := strconv.ParseFloat(fields[0], 64) => vacuum, err := strconv.ParseFloat(fields[0], 65) :: strconv.ParseFloat reads every bit size but 32 as 64
planet/methane.go methaneRows: coefficient, err := strconv.ParseFloat(fields[2], 64) => coefficient, err := strconv.ParseFloat(fields[2], 65) :: strconv.ParseFloat reads every bit size but 32 as 64
set/planet.go layer: math.Abs(sum-1) > 1e-9 => math.Abs(sum-1) >= 1e-9 :: differs only when the shares miss one by exactly 1e-9, which no sum of float shares lands on
planet/rings.go shadowed: if slant == 0 { return 1 } => if slant == 0 { } :: with the star in the ring's plane the division gives an endless or no number, which no ring's radii hold, so every point comes out unshadowed either way
set/planet.go ring: math.Abs(sum-1) > 1e-9 => math.Abs(sum-1) >= 1e-9 :: differs only when the make-up misses one by exactly 1e-9, which no sum of float shares lands on

# carried into slayer: interfaces/file
interfaces/file/file.go Block: if pixel <= 0 { return 1, 1 } => if pixel <= 0 { } :: for a width of nought or less every block rounds below one and is passed over, so the loop's own 1 by 1 is returned all the same
interfaces/file/file.go Block: pixel <= 0 => pixel < 0 :: a width of exactly nought rounds every block to nought, which the loop passes over, so it is 1 by 1 either way
interfaces/file/file.go Block: w, h, best := 1, 1, math.Inf(1) => w, h, best := 1, 1, math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf of any positive number is the same positive infinity
interfaces/file/file.go Block: e < best-1e-9 => e <= best-1e-9 :: the two differ only when a shape is nearer than the best by exactly a billionth, which two shapes of whole numbers up to 8 never are
interfaces/file/file.go Grow: y < out.Bounds().Dy() => y <= out.Bounds().Dy() :: setting a pixel past an image's edge does nothing to it, so one row more drawn past the edge leaves the picture as it was
interfaces/file/file.go Grow: x < out.Bounds().Dx() => x <= out.Bounds().Dx() :: setting a pixel past an image's edge does nothing to it, so one column more drawn past the edge leaves the picture as it was
interfaces/file/file.go Block: bh++ => bh-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
interfaces/file/file.go Grow: y++ => y-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
interfaces/file/file.go Grow: x++ => x-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out

# carried into slayer: maths
maths/smooth.go Smooth: x <= a => x < a :: at x equal to a the ease below gives nought, as the step does
maths/smooth.go Smooth: x >= b => x > b :: at x equal to b the ease below gives one, as the step does
maths/space.go Everywhere: inf := math.Inf(1) => inf := math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf of any positive number is the same positive infinity
maths/space.go Hit: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: on a miss, enter and leave are never read; every caller asks ok first
maths/space.go Hit: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: on a miss, enter and leave are never read; every caller asks ok first
maths/space.go Hit: t0 > t1 => t0 >= t1 :: swapping two equal numbers leaves them as they were
maths/space.go Hit: math.IsInf(enter, 1) => math.IsInf(enter, 2) :: math.IsInf with any positive sign asks after positive infinity
maths/space.go LookAtUp: math.Abs(back.Dot(up)) > 0.999 => math.Abs(back.Dot(up)) >= 0.999 :: the two differ only at a dot of exactly 0.999, where up and minus z are each a sound top of the view
maths/space.go Upright: math.Abs(y.Dot(e)) > 0.9 => math.Abs(y.Dot(e)) >= 0.9 :: the two differ only at a dot of exactly 0.9, where x and z are each a sound side to build from

# carried into slayer: film
film/film.go Light: return s.Toward.Unit(), math.Inf(1), s.Strength => return s.Toward.Unit(), math.Inf(2), s.Strength :: math.Inf of any positive number is the same positive infinity
film/film.go Light: return maths.Vec{}, math.Inf(1), f.Strength.Scale(facing) => return maths.Vec{}, math.Inf(2), f.Strength.Scale(facing) :: math.Inf of any positive number is the same positive infinity
film/film.go Light: up = maths.V(0, 1, 0) => up = maths.V(0, 2, 0) :: up is made one unit long before it is used, so any length of the world's up is the same up
film/film.go edge: off <= inner => off < inner :: at off equal to inner the smooth step below gives one, as the plain branch does
film/film.go edge: s.Soft <= 0 => s.Soft < 0 :: with no soft edge inner is the cone, so every angle short of the cone is already at or inside inner
film/sensor.go Ray: !(p.Wide >= 0 && p.Wide < math.Pi) => !(p.Wide > 0 && p.Wide < math.Pi) :: a Wide of nought is set to nought, which wide reads as the default either way
film/sensor.go Ray: !(p.Pixel >= 0) => !(p.Pixel > 0) :: a Pixel of nought is set to nought, which pixel reads as square either way
film/sensor.go Ray: p.Pixel = 0 => p.Pixel = 1 :: pixel reads nought as square, which one is
film/film.go Light: off := math.Acos(math.Max(-1, math.Min(1, toward.Neg().Dot(s.Aim.Unit())))) => off := math.Acos(math.Max(-1, math.Min(2, toward.Neg().Dot(s.Aim.Unit())))) :: not equivalent, and no test here reaches it: the clamp keeps a dot that rounds past one from Acos, which would give NaN. a search found such a dot, aim and point both (1, 0, 5), in a program of its own; inside Light the compiler joins the arithmetic otherwise and the same inputs round to one. kept, since the clamp costs nothing and the rounding is real

# carried into slayer: render/frame
render/frame/frame.go New: inf := float32(math.Inf(1)) => inf := float32(math.Inf(2)) :: math.Inf of any positive number is the same positive infinity
scene/shape/mesh.go NewMesh: finite(n) && n != (maths.Vec{}) => finite(n) || n != (maths.Vec{}) :: a normal that is not finite has a part that is no number or endless, and so has its Unit; check refuses either in the same words, and a finite normal takes the same path both ways
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: runs := make(map[edge]int, 3*len(m.Faces)) => runs := make(map[edge]int, 3/len(m.Faces)) :: a map's size is a hint only, and NewMesh refuses a mesh of no faces before wind, so the division is never by nought
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: runs := make(map[edge]int, 3*len(m.Faces)) => runs := make(map[edge]int, 4*len(m.Faces)) :: a map's size is a hint only
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]] => lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[1][0]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]] :: the box starts at any corner of the mesh and takes the least and most of every corner after, so it ends the same; a closed mesh, the only kind that reaches here, has two faces at least
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]] => lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][1]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]] :: the box starts at any corner of the mesh and takes the least and most of every corner after, so it ends the same
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]] => lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[1][0]] :: the box starts at any corner of the mesh and takes the least and most of every corner after, so it ends the same; a closed mesh, the only kind that reaches here, has two faces at least
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]] => lo, hi := m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][0]], m.Vertices[m.Faces[0][1]] :: the box starts at any corner of the mesh and takes the least and most of every corner after, so it ends the same
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: piece[root(id[f[2]])] = root(id[f[0]]) => piece[root(id[f[2]])] = root(id[f[1]]) :: the line before has just joined f[1] to f[0]'s piece, so f[1]'s root is f[0]'s
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: volume[root(id[f[0]])] += a.Dot(b.Cross(c)) => volume[root(id[f[1]])] += a.Dot(b.Cross(c)) :: a face's three corners are joined into one piece before any volume is weighed, so its corners share one root
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: volume[root(id[f[0]])] < 0 => volume[root(id[f[1]])] < 0 :: a face's three corners are joined into one piece before any volume is weighed, so its corners share one root
scene/shape/mesh.go package: look = maths.V(0.48, 0.6, 0.64) => look = maths.V(1.96, 0.6, 0.64) :: Inside counts the faces a ray crosses, met watertight, so any way out that lies along no face's plane counts the same; this one lines up with no axis either, and its length is not read
scene/shape/mesh.go package: look = maths.V(0.48, 0.6, 0.64) => look = maths.V(0.48, 2.2, 0.64) :: Inside counts the faces a ray crosses, met watertight, so any way out that lies along no face's plane counts the same; this one lines up with no axis either, and its length is not read
scene/shape/mesh.go package: look = maths.V(0.48, 0.6, 0.64) => look = maths.V(0.48, 0.6, 2.2800000000000002) :: Inside counts the faces a ray crosses, met watertight, so any way out that lies along no face's plane counts the same; this one lines up with no axis either, and its length is not read
scene/shape/mesh.go wind: piece[i] != i => piece[i] == i :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
scene/shape/shape.go Hit: return 0, false => return 1, false :: Hit promises a distance only where it meets; on a miss the number is not read, and Plane gives its own t on a miss
scene/shape/shape.go Hit: if disc < 0 { return 0, false } => if disc < 0 { } :: below nought the root is no number, which passes no comparison, so the loop meets nothing and it is the same miss
scene/shape/shape.go Span: if disc < 0 { return 0, 0, false } => if disc < 0 { } :: below nought the root is no number, so enter and leave are no number and leave > enter is false: the same miss
scene/shape/shape.go Span: disc < 0 => disc <= 0 :: at exactly nought the ray only touches: enter and leave are one number, leave > enter is false, and it is a miss either way
scene/shape/shape.go Span: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: Span promises an entry and a leaving only where it crosses; on a miss they are not read
scene/shape/shape.go Span: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: Span promises an entry and a leaving only where it crosses; on a miss they are not read
scene/shape/shape.go Hit: if r.Dir.Y == 0 { return 0, false } => if r.Dir.Y == 0 { } :: a level ray's t is endless or no number, which passes neither near nor far, so it misses all the same; its distance on a miss is not read
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: d1 <= 0 && d2 <= 0 => d1 < 0 && d2 <= 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: d1 <= 0 && d2 <= 0 => d1 <= 0 && d2 < 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: d3 >= 0 && d4 <= d3 => d3 > 0 && d4 <= d3 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: d3 >= 0 && d4 <= d3 => d3 >= 0 && d4 < d3 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: vc <= 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 <= 0 => vc < 0 && d1 >= 0 && d3 <= 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
