scene/shape/triangle.go closest: d6 >= 0 && d5 <= d6 => d6 >= 0 && d5 < d6 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 => vb < 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 => vb <= 0 && d2 > 0 && d6 <= 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 <= 0 => vb <= 0 && d2 >= 0 && d6 < 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 => va < 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 => va <= 0 && d4-d3 > 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go closest: va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 >= 0 => va <= 0 && d4-d3 >= 0 && d5-d6 > 0 :: it changes the branch only where p lies on the line between two of the regions, a corner's and a side's or a side's and the face's, and there both regions' formulas give the same point, to rounding
scene/shape/triangle.go ready: math.Abs(d[1]) > math.Abs(d[kz]) => math.Abs(d[1]) >= math.Abs(d[kz]) :: on a tie both axes are ones the ray runs most along; ready may take either, and the test is watertight about either
scene/shape/triangle.go ready: math.Abs(d[2]) > math.Abs(d[kz]) => math.Abs(d[2]) >= math.Abs(d[kz]) :: on a tie both axes are ones the ray runs most along; ready may take either, and the test is watertight about either
scene/shape/triangle.go ready: d[kz] < 0 => d[kz] <= 0 :: the part a ray runs most along is nought only when it runs nowhere, and then every shear is no number and nothing is met, whichever way the axes are swapped
scene/shape/triangle.go meet: return 0, false, false => return 1, false, false :: meet promises a distance only where it meets; on a miss the number is not read
scene/shape/triangle.go meet: if whole == 0 { return 0, false, false } => if whole == 0 { } :: u, v and w share a sign here, so whole is nought only when all three are, and then d is nought over nought, no number, which no range holds: the same miss
scene/shape/triangle.go meet: return d, whole > 0, d > near && d < far => return d, whole >= 0, d > near && d < far :: whole is never nought here, the line before having returned for it
scene/shape/triangle.go side: px > qx => px >= qx :: reached only when the edge's two ends land on one point across the ray: the edge runs along the ray, so the face is seen edge on and meet misses it by its signs or by its whole being nought, either way; or the edge has no length, a face NewMesh refuses
scene/shape/triangle.go side: px < qx => px <= qx :: reached only when the edge's two ends land on one point across the ray: the edge runs along the ray, so the face is seen edge on and meet misses it by its signs or by its whole being nought, either way; or the edge has no length, a face NewMesh refuses
scene/shape/triangle.go side: return 0 => return 1 :: reached only when the edge's two ends land on one point across the ray: the edge runs along the ray, so the face is seen edge on and meet misses it by its signs or by its whole being nought, either way; or the edge has no length, a face NewMesh refuses
scene/shape/meshtree.go build: b.mids[i] = u.Add(v).Add(w).Scale(1.0 / 3) => b.mids[i] = u.Add(v).Add(w).Scale(1.0 * 3) :: one factor for every middle leaves each comparison of middles, which axis they spread most on and each share of a spread as it was, but for a middle within rounding of the edge between two bins, whose face falls in the next bin over: a cut shifted by one face, which neither the answers nor the bounds on leaves and depth care about
scene/shape/meshtree.go build: b.mids[i] = u.Add(v).Add(w).Scale(1.0 / 3) => b.mids[i] = u.Add(v).Add(w).Scale(3 / 3) :: one factor for every middle leaves each comparison of middles, which axis they spread most on and each share of a spread as it was, but for a middle within rounding of the edge between two bins, whose face falls in the next bin over: a cut shifted by one face, which neither the answers nor the bounds on leaves and depth care about
scene/shape/meshtree.go build: b.mids[i] = u.Add(v).Add(w).Scale(1.0 / 3) => b.mids[i] = u.Add(v).Add(w).Scale(1.0 / 4) :: one factor for every middle leaves each comparison of middles, which axis they spread most on and each share of a spread as it was, but for a middle within rounding of the edge between two bins, whose face falls in the next bin over: a cut shifted by one face, which neither the answers nor the bounds on leaves and depth care about
scene/shape/meshtree.go split: depth < deepest && along(mids.Max, axis) > along(mids.Min, axis) => depth < deepest && along(mids.Max, axis) >= along(mids.Min, axis) :: without a spread of middles the width is nought and each bin is int(0/0), which the language leaves to the machine: on arm64 it is 0, so every face is in bin 0, no cut is found and cheapest returns lo, as when it is not called; on a machine where it is a negative integer, as the language allows, the bin is out of range and the change panics, which TestFacesWithOneMiddleAreHalvedByCount would see there
scene/shape/meshtree.go split: return along(b.mids[part[x]], axis) < along(b.mids[part[y]], axis) => return along(b.mids[part[x]], axis) <= along(b.mids[part[y]], axis) :: sort.Slice does not keep faces of one middle in the order given, with either test; with <= the middles still come out in order, as a run over 400000 arrays of up to 300 with many equal keys showed, so only which of two faces of one middle falls in which half differs, and that is not promised
scene/shape/meshtree.go cheapest: k > 0 => k >= 0 :: the second loop reads above and aboveArea from 1 up: the entries at 0 are written and never read
scene/shape/meshtree.go cheapest: best, at := math.Inf(1), 0 => best, at := math.Inf(2), 0 :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
scene/shape/meshtree.go cheapest: best, at := math.Inf(1), 0 => best, at := math.Inf(1), 1 :: the first cut tried, at 1, has a face below it, in bin 0, and one above, in bin 15, and costs a number under endless unless area times count passes the largest double, which takes millions of faces across 1e150; so at is set there whatever it began as, and were every cost endless, a cut at 1 is still a cut of bin 0 from the rest
scene/shape/meshtree.go cheapest: if c == 0 || above[k] == 0 { continue } => if c == 0 || above[k] == 0 { } :: bin 0 holds the face with the least middle, whose offset from it is nought, and bin 15 the face with the greatest, whose share of the spread is exactly one and is held to 15; so every cut has a face below it and a face above, and this guard never fires
scene/shape/meshtree.go cheapest: c == 0 || above[k] == 0 => c == 0 && above[k] == 0 :: bin 0 holds the face with the least middle, whose offset from it is nought, and bin 15 the face with the greatest, whose share of the spread is exactly one and is held to 15; so every cut has a face below it and a face above, and this guard never fires
scene/shape/meshtree.go cheapest: cost < best => cost <= best :: two cuts of exactly one cost are equally cheap, and which is taken is not promised, only that the halves are cheapest to search
scene/shape/meshtree.go cheapest: at == 0 || (n <= leafMost && 1+best/area(box) >= float64(n)) => at == 0 || (n <= leafMost && 1+best/area(box) > float64(n)) :: a leaf and a cut priced exactly alike cost the same to search, and which is made is not promised
scene/shape/meshtree.go empty: in, out := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-1) => in, out := math.Inf(2), math.Inf(-1) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
scene/shape/meshtree.go empty: in, out := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-1) => in, out := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign below nought is negative, so Inf(-2) is Inf(-1)
scene/shape/meshtree.go longest: d.X >= d.Y => d.X > d.Y :: on a tie the other axis taken is also one the box is longest along
scene/shape/meshtree.go longest: d.X >= d.Z => d.X > d.Z :: on a tie the other axis taken is also one the box is longest along
scene/shape/meshtree.go longest: d.Y >= d.Z => d.Y > d.Z :: on a tie the other axis taken is also one the box is longest along
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: if _, ok := q.box(&nodes[0].box, near, far); !ok { return 0, -1, false } => if _, ok := q.box(&nodes[0].box, near, far); !ok { } :: a ray the root's box turns away is turned away by both boxes of the root's halves, which lie inside it, or meets no face of a root that is one leaf, all inside it; so without the test the walk ends in the same miss, a box test later
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: top, n, best, which := 0, int32(0), far, -1 => top, n, best, which := 1, int32(0), far, -1 :: the first slot then holds node 0 at nought, which is popped last and walks the tree again with the best already found: it finds nothing nearer, and visits only nodes the first walk visited, so the answer is the same and the work at most twice
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: top, n, best, which := 0, int32(0), far, -1 => top, n, best, which := 0, int32(0), far, -2 :: which is read only against nought: -2 is as far below as -1
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: tb < ta => tb <= ta :: two boxes entered at one distance, or as far, neither is nearer, so which is searched first is not promised, and what is found is as near either way
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: n = -1 => n = -2 :: n is held below nought only to say no node is waiting, and is read only against nought: -2 is as far below as -1
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: top > 0 && n < 0 => top > 0 && n <= 0 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: top > 0 && n < 0 => top > 0 && n < 1 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: stack[top].d <= best => stack[top].d < best :: a face is met no sooner than the box it is in is entered, the box being let out by a hair past the face, which is the margin for rounding, and meet takes only a face nearer than best; so a box entered at best holds none
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: n < 0 && which < 0 => n <= 0 && which < 0 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: n < 0 && which < 0 => n < 1 && which < 0 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: n < 0 => n <= 0 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go first: n < 0 => n < 1 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go winding: _, ok := q.box(&nd.box, 0, math.Inf(1)) => _, ok := q.box(&nd.box, 0, math.Inf(2)) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
scene/shape/meshtree.go winding: _, front, ok := q.meet(&m.tris[i], 0, math.Inf(1)) => _, front, ok := q.meet(&m.tris[i], 0, math.Inf(2)) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: top, n, best, which := 0, int32(0), math.Inf(1), 0 => top, n, best, which := 1, int32(0), math.Inf(1), 0 :: the first slot then holds node 0 at nought, which is popped last and walks the tree again with the best distance already found: it finds nothing nearer, and visits only nodes the first walk visited, so the answer is the same and the work at most twice
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: top, n, best, which := 0, int32(0), math.Inf(1), 0 => top, n, best, which := 0, int32(0), math.Inf(2), 0 :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: d2 < best => d2 <= best :: a face as near p as the nearest so far is as near as it, and which of two equally near faces is named is not promised
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: db < da => db <= da :: two boxes entered at one distance, or as far, neither is nearer, so which is searched first is not promised, and what is found is as near either way
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: db < best => db <= best :: a face is no nearer than the box it is in is far, the box being let out by a hair past the face, which is the margin for rounding, and a face replaces the best only when nearer; so a box as far as the best holds none
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: da < best => da <= best :: a face is no nearer than the box it is in is far, the box being let out by a hair past the face, which is the margin for rounding, and a face replaces the best only when nearer; so a box as far as the best holds none
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: n = -1 => n = -2 :: n is held below nought only to say no node is waiting, and is read only against nought: -2 is as far below as -1
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: top > 0 && n < 0 => top > 0 && n <= 0 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: top > 0 && n < 0 => top > 0 && n < 1 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: stack[top].d < best => stack[top].d <= best :: a face is no nearer than the box it is in is far, the box being let out by a hair past the face, which is the margin for rounding, and a face replaces the best only when nearer; so a box as far as the best holds none
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: n < 0 => n <= 0 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go nearest: n < 0 => n < 1 :: n is -1 or a node taken off the stack, and only children are put on the stack, none of which is the root: n is never nought, so < 0, <= 0 and < 1 say the same
scene/shape/meshtree.go slab: inv < 0 => inv <= 0 :: an inverse is nought only for a part endlessly large, which a ray does not have; it is never exactly nought
scene/shape/meshtree.go slab: t > enter => t >= enter :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number
scene/shape/meshtree.go slab: t < leave => t <= leave :: when the slab is as far as the bound it is held against, taking it or keeping the bound is one number

scene/medium/aurora.go Density: up < a.Low || up > a.High => up <= a.Low || up > a.High :: at up equal to Low, h is nought and Smooth(0, 0.1, h) is nought, so the profile and the density are nought there, and the curtains scale a nought by a number: nought either way
scene/medium/aurora.go Density: oval < 1e-3 => oval <= 1e-3 :: no oval is exactly 1e-3, so a cut under it and a cut at it sort every oval the same; the oval is exp(-off*off) and the exp of no double within two thousand steps of ln 1000 is that double, as measured for math.Exp on arm64 (a measurement, not a proof); TestNoOvalIsExactlyAThousandth fails on a machine where one is
scene/medium/aurora.go Density: a.Curtains > 0 => a.Curtains >= 0 :: with Curtains nought the factor is max(0, 1 + 0*(2*rays+folds)), which is one for any rays and folds Fractal gives, so multiplying by it changes nothing
scene/medium/disc.go Span: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: Span promises an entry and a leaving only where the ray is in the disc; on a miss they are not read, and every caller in the tree (trace/stage.go, render/trace/trace.go, render/shortcut/baked.go) asks ok first
scene/medium/disc.go Span: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: Span promises an entry and a leaving only where the ray is in the disc; on a miss they are not read, and every caller in the tree (trace/stage.go, render/trace/trace.go, render/shortcut/baked.go) asks ok first
scene/medium/disc.go Span: t0 > t1 => t0 >= t1 :: swapping two equal numbers leaves them as they were
scene/medium/disc.go Span: disc < 0 => disc <= 0 :: at exactly nought the ray only touches the cylinder: the root is nought, enter is no less than -b and leave no more, so leave > enter is false and it is a miss either way
scene/medium/disc.go Span: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: Span promises an entry and a leaving only where the ray is in the disc; on a miss they are not read, and every caller in the tree (trace/stage.go, render/trace/trace.go, render/shortcut/baked.go) asks ok first
scene/medium/disc.go Span: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: Span promises an entry and a leaving only where the ray is in the disc; on a miss they are not read, and every caller in the tree (trace/stage.go, render/trace/trace.go, render/shortcut/baked.go) asks ok first
scene/medium/disc.go Density: h <= 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) => h <= 0 && d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) :: each of the two conditions returns nought on its own further down, for a Dense and a Profile that are numbers: at a Thick of nought or less every point fails |y| > h or has an ease Smooth(0, 0.1h, h-|y|) of nought; with Outer no more than Inner every point fails r < Inner or r > Outer, or is at Inner = Outer where t is nought over nought and profile gives nought for it
scene/medium/disc.go Density: h <= 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) => h < 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) :: at a Thick of exactly nought every point fails |y| > h or has an ease Smooth(0, 0.1h, h-|y|) of nought, so the density is nought further down, for a Dense and a Profile that are numbers
scene/medium/disc.go Density: h <= 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) => h <= 0 || d.Outer < d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) :: with Outer equal to Inner every point fails r < Inner or r > Outer, or is at Inner = Outer where t is nought over nought and profile gives nought for a t that is no number, so the density is nought further down, for a Dense and a Profile that are numbers
scene/medium/disc.go Density: h <= 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) => h <= 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y-p.Z) :: a part that is no number makes the sum none with either sign; the two sums differ only for endless parts, and an endless x or z makes r > Outer and an endless y makes |y| > h, which return nought on the next test
scene/medium/disc.go Density: h <= 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X+p.Y+p.Z) => h <= 0 || d.Outer <= d.Inner || math.IsNaN(p.X-p.Y+p.Z) :: a part that is no number makes the sum none with either sign; the two sums differ only for endless parts, and an endless x or z makes r > Outer and an endless y makes |y| > h, which return nought on the next test
scene/medium/disc.go Density: r < d.Inner || r > d.Outer || math.Abs(p.Y) > h => r < d.Inner || r > d.Outer || math.Abs(p.Y) >= h :: at |y| equal to h the ease Smooth(0, 0.1h, h-|y|) is Smooth at nought, which is nought, so the density is nought there either way, for a Dense and a Profile that are numbers
scene/medium/disc.go Density: d.Grain > 0 => d.Grain >= 0 :: with Grain nought the factor is 1 - 0*(0.5+0.5*ring), which is one for any ring Fractal gives, so multiplying by it changes nothing
scene/medium/disc.go profile: x := math.Max(0, math.Min(1, t)) * float64(n-1) => x := math.Max(0, math.Min(2, t)) * float64(n-1) :: for a t of one or less the two bounds are the same number; for a t above one, x is at least n-1 under either, so i >= n-1 and the last entry is returned, as it is for a t of one
scene/medium/jet.go Span: if !ok { return 0, 0, false } => if !ok { } :: when slab misses, the range it hands on has near at or past far, and cylinder over such a range gives an enter no less than near and a leave no more than far, so enter < leave is false and the same miss is returned
scene/medium/jet.go Density: if j.Length <= 0 { return 0 } => if j.Length <= 0 { } :: a Length of nought or less makes y >= Length true for y, an absolute value, so the next line returns nought; the two differ only at a point that is not a number, of which the package promises nothing
scene/medium/jet.go Density: j.Length <= 0 => j.Length < 0 :: a Length of exactly nought makes y >= Length true for y, an absolute value, so the next line returns nought; the two differ only at a point that is not a number, of which the package promises nothing
scene/medium/jet.go Density: y >= j.Length || y < j.From => y > j.Length || y < j.From :: at y equal to Length the factor 1 - y/Length is nought, so the density is nought there either way
scene/medium/jet.go Density: j.Knots > 0 => j.Knots >= 0 :: with Knots nought the factor is max(0, 1 + 0*2*n), which is one for any n Fractal gives, so multiplying by it changes nothing
scene/medium/jet.go slab: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: slab is called only by Jet.Span and Tail.Span, which return a miss as soon as ok is false; on a miss its numbers are not read
scene/medium/jet.go slab: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: slab is called only by Jet.Span and Tail.Span, which return a miss as soon as ok is false; on a miss its numbers are not read
scene/medium/jet.go slab: a > b => a >= b :: swapping two equal numbers leaves them as they were
scene/medium/jet.go cylinder: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: cylinder is called only by Jet.Span and Tail.Span, which return a miss as soon as ok is false; on a miss its numbers are not read
scene/medium/jet.go cylinder: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: cylinder is called only by Jet.Span and Tail.Span, which return a miss as soon as ok is false; on a miss its numbers are not read
scene/medium/jet.go cylinder: if disc < 0 { return 0, 0, false } => if disc < 0 { } :: below nought the root is no number, so t1, t2, enter and leave are none, and enter < leave is false: the same miss
scene/medium/jet.go cylinder: disc < 0 => disc <= 0 :: at exactly nought the ray only touches the cylinder: t1 and t2 are one number, enter is no less than it and leave no more, so enter < leave is false and it is a miss either way
scene/medium/jet.go cylinder: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: cylinder is called only by Jet.Span and Tail.Span, which return a miss as soon as ok is false; on a miss its numbers are not read
scene/medium/jet.go cylinder: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: cylinder is called only by Jet.Span and Tail.Span, which return a miss as soon as ok is false; on a miss its numbers are not read
scene/medium/jet.go probed: first, last := -1, -1 => first, last := -2, -1 :: first is only tested for below nought, and the first probe with gas sets it, so any value below nought is the same unset
scene/medium/jet.go probed: first, last := -1, -1 => first, last := -1, +1 :: last is read only once first is set, and the first probe with gas has set last by then; its starting value is never read
scene/medium/jet.go probed: first, last := -1, -1 => first, last := -1, -2 :: last is read only once first is set, and the first probe with gas has set last by then; its starting value is never read
scene/medium/medium.go Density: if d == 0 { return 0 } => if d == 0 { } :: a d of nought makes d times the noise's factor nought, for a factor that is a number, which 1 - thin*(0.5+0.5n) is for any n Fractal gives and a thin kept from nought to one; the early return only saves the noise
scene/medium/medium.go Density: g.Radius <= 0 || g.Depth <= 0 => g.Radius < 0 || g.Depth <= 0 :: a Radius of exactly nought makes below, Radius less the length, nought or less at every point, which returns nought on the next test; the two differ only at a point that is not a number, of which the package promises nothing
scene/medium/medium.go Density: if below <= 0 { return 0 } => if below <= 0 { } :: a below of nought or less is an x at or under a for Smooth(0, Depth, below), which is nought, so Thick times it is nought for a Thick that is a number; the early return only saves the arithmetic
scene/medium/medium.go Density: below <= 0 => below < 0 :: a below of exactly nought is an x at a for Smooth(0, Depth, below), which is nought, so Thick times it is nought for a Thick that is a number
scene/medium/plume.go Density: h <= 0 || h >= 1 => h < 0 || h >= 1 :: differs only on the plane y = 0, the floor of Bounds, where the density is nought as first drawn; Medium promises nought outside Bounds and nothing on its edge
scene/medium/plume.go Density: h <= 0 || h >= 1 => h <= 0 || h > 1 :: differs only on the plane y = Height, the top of Bounds, where the density is nought as first drawn; Medium promises nought outside Bounds and nothing on its edge
scene/medium/tail.go Span: enter, leave, ok := slab(t.Length/2, maths.Ray{At: r.At.Add(maths.V(0, -t.Length/2, 0)), Dir: r.Dir}, near, far) => enter, leave, ok := slab(t.Length/2, maths.Ray{At: r.At.Add(maths.V(1, -t.Length/2, 0)), Dir: r.Dir}, near, far) :: slab reads only the ray's y, so a shift of the ray along x or z changes nothing
scene/medium/tail.go Span: enter, leave, ok := slab(t.Length/2, maths.Ray{At: r.At.Add(maths.V(0, -t.Length/2, 0)), Dir: r.Dir}, near, far) => enter, leave, ok := slab(t.Length/2, maths.Ray{At: r.At.Add(maths.V(0, -t.Length/2, 1)), Dir: r.Dir}, near, far) :: slab reads only the ray's y, so a shift of the ray along x or z changes nothing
scene/medium/tail.go Span: if !ok { return 0, 0, false } => if !ok { } :: when slab misses, the range it hands on has near at or past far, and cylinder over such a range gives an enter no less than near and a leave no more than far, so enter < leave is false and the same miss is returned
scene/medium/tail.go Density: t.Length <= 0 || p.Y < 0 || p.Y >= t.Length => t.Length < 0 || p.Y < 0 || p.Y >= t.Length :: a Length of exactly nought makes p.Y >= Length true for every y of nought or more and p.Y < 0 true for the rest, so the point is returned as nought; the two differ only at a point that is not a number, of which the package promises nothing
scene/medium/tail.go Density: t.Length <= 0 || p.Y < 0 || p.Y >= t.Length => t.Length <= 0 || p.Y < 0 || p.Y > t.Length :: at y equal to Length the factor 1 - y/Length is nought, so the density is nought there either way
scene/medium/clouds.go Density: if patch <= 0 { return 0 } => if patch <= 0 { } :: a patch of nought is Smooth giving exactly nought, and Thick times nought times the height's share is nought for a Thick that is a number; the early return only saves the arithmetic
scene/medium/clouds.go Density: patch <= 0 => patch < 0 :: Smooth is never below nought, so the changed test never returns; a patch of nought goes on to Thick times nought times the height's share, which is nought for a Thick that is a number
scene/medium/pillars.go columns: p.Count <= 0 => p.Count < 0 :: a Count of nought makes an empty list where nought or less makes none; its only readers, Tips and Density, range over it (Density returns before it for a Count of nought), and an empty list and none are the same to a range
scene/medium/pillars.go Density: at.Y < top => at.Y <= top :: at y equal to top the ease 1 - Smooth(top - 0.08*Height, top, y) is nought, so the wall is nought there and most is unchanged by the max
scene/medium/pillars.go Density: at.Y > from => at.Y >= from :: at y equal to from, u is nought and sqrt(1 - u*u) is one, so the dome leaves r as it was
scene/medium/pillars.go Density: p.Filaments > 0 && got > 0 => p.Filaments >= 0 && got > 0 :: with Filaments nought the factor is 1 - 0*min(1, max(0, 0.5-m)), which is one for any m Fractal gives, so got is unchanged
scene/medium/pillars.go Density: p.Filaments > 0 && got > 0 => p.Filaments > 0 && got >= 0 :: a got of nought is nought times the streak factor, which is nought and is no more than the fog it is held against by the max; got is never below nought, so only that case is added
scene/medium/jet.go probed: i++ => i-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
# maths/hexes
maths/hexes/lattice.go roundHex: dq > dr => dq >= dr :: where two of the three rounding errors are equal they have the same sign and size, since the three come to a whole number and none is over a half, so correcting either gives a tile exactly as near the point; the point is in both, and the package promises the nearest, not which of two. this is the algebra, not a run
maths/hexes/lattice.go roundHex: dq > ds => dq >= ds :: where two of the three rounding errors are equal they have the same sign and size, since the three come to a whole number and none is over a half, so correcting either gives a tile exactly as near the point; the point is in both, and the package promises the nearest, not which of two. this is the algebra, not a run
maths/hexes/lattice.go roundHex: dr > ds => dr >= ds :: where two of the three rounding errors are equal they have the same sign and size, since the three come to a whole number and none is over a half, so correcting either gives a tile exactly as near the point; the point is in both, and the package promises the nearest, not which of two. this is the algebra, not a run
maths/hexes/lattice.go around: out[i] = [2]int{c + s[0], r + s[1]} => out[i] = [2]int{c + s[0], r - s[1]} :: the steps of each row parity come in pairs that differ only in the sign of the row step, so negating it gives the same six tiles in another order; every caller sorts them or takes the least over them
maths/hexes/lattice.go fromEdge: best := math.Inf(1) => best := math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
maths/hexes/lattice.go Neighbours: return f.ID(out[a]) < f.ID(out[b]) => return f.ID(out[a]) <= f.ID(out[b]) :: the lists are at most six long, which sort.Slice orders by insertion, asking only about two different tiles, whose ids differ, so <= orders as <
maths/hexes/lattice.go Tiles: r++ => r-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
maths/hexes/lattice.go Tiles: c++ => c-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
maths/hexes/lattice.go Neighbours: dup = dup || have == t => dup = dup && have == t :: dup is never true: the six steps are six different tiles, and wrap keeps the three columns c-1, c and c+1 apart because Around is at least six, so no tile is made twice and dup stays false either way
maths/hexes/lattice.go Neighbours: return b.ID(out[x]) < b.ID(out[y]) => return b.ID(out[x]) <= b.ID(out[y]) :: the lists are at most six long, which sort.Slice orders by insertion, asking only about two different tiles, whose ids differ, so <= orders as <
maths/hexes/lattice.go Edge: u-cu > circ/2 => u-cu >= circ/2 :: at exactly half a turn the point is as far one way round as the other, and the six neighbours come in pairs either side of the middle, so the edge is the same whichever way it is taken, to rounding
maths/hexes/lattice.go Edge: cu-u > circ/2 => cu-u >= circ/2 :: at exactly half a turn the point is as far one way round as the other, and the six neighbours come in pairs either side of the middle, so the edge is the same whichever way it is taken, to rounding
maths/hexes/lattice.go ID: n > 1<<32 => n >= 1<<32 :: at exactly 2^32 columns row*n + column is row<<32 + column, which is what the pair of thirty-two bits gives for every column below 2^32, so the number is the same either way
maths/hexes/lattice.go Tiles: r++ => r-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
maths/hexes/lattice.go Tiles: c++ => c-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: lo, hi := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-1) => lo, hi := math.Inf(2), math.Inf(-1) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: lo, hi := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-1) => lo, hi := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign below nought is negative, so Inf(-2) is Inf(-1)
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: lz, hz2 := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-1) => lz, hz2 := math.Inf(2), math.Inf(-1) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: lz, hz2 := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-1) => lz, hz2 := math.Inf(1), math.Inf(-2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign below nought is negative, so Inf(-2) is Inf(-1)
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: []Tile{{0, 0, 0}, {0, a - 1, 0}, {0, 0, d - 1}, {0, a - 1, d - 1}} => []Tile{{1, 0, 0}, {0, a - 1, 0}, {0, 0, d - 1}, {0, a - 1, d - 1}} :: Flat.Centre reads a tile's I and J and never its Face
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: []Tile{{0, 0, 0}, {0, a - 1, 0}, {0, 0, d - 1}, {0, a - 1, d - 1}} => []Tile{{0, 0, 0}, {1, a - 1, 0}, {0, 0, d - 1}, {0, a - 1, d - 1}} :: Flat.Centre reads a tile's I and J and never its Face
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: []Tile{{0, 0, 0}, {0, a - 1, 0}, {0, 0, d - 1}, {0, a - 1, d - 1}} => []Tile{{0, 0, 0}, {0, a - 1, 0}, {1, 0, d - 1}, {0, a - 1, d - 1}} :: Flat.Centre reads a tile's I and J and never its Face
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: []Tile{{0, 0, 0}, {0, a - 1, 0}, {0, 0, d - 1}, {0, a - 1, d - 1}} => []Tile{{0, 0, 0}, {0, a - 1, 0}, {0, 0, d - 1}, {1, a - 1, d - 1}} :: Flat.Centre reads a tile's I and J and never its Face
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: return maths.V((lo+hi)/2, 0, (lz+hz2)/2), (hi - lo) / 2, (hz2 - lz) / 2 => return maths.V((lo+hi)/2, 0, (lz+hz2)/2), (hi + lo) / 2, (hz2 - lz) / 2 :: lo is nought, the first corner's middle being the origin and every other at or past it, so hi+lo and hi-lo are both hi
maths/hexes/lattice.go bounds: return maths.V((lo+hi)/2, 0, (lz+hz2)/2), (hi - lo) / 2, (hz2 - lz) / 2 => return maths.V((lo+hi)/2, 0, (lz+hz2)/2), (hi - lo) / 2, (hz2 + lz) / 2 :: lz is nought, the first corner's middle being the origin and every other at or past it, so hz2+lz and hz2-lz are both hz2
maths/hexes/sphere.go n: t.N < 1 => t.N <= 1 :: at N of 1 the answer is 1 whichever way the line reads, and the change reaches no other N
maths/hexes/sphere.go n: t.N < 1 => t.N < 2 :: at N of 1 the answer is 1 whichever way the line reads, and the change reaches no other N
maths/hexes/sphere.go SphereFor: if math.IsNaN(radians) { return Sphere{N: 1} } => if math.IsNaN(radians) { } :: int of NaN is left to the machine: it is 0 on arm64, where this was run, and the lowest int on amd64 by its hardware, not run here; max(1, n) then gives 1 as the guard does, so on those two the guard changes nothing
maths/hexes/sphere.go SphereFor: radians <= edge/finest => radians < edge/finest :: at radians of exactly edge/finest, edge/radians comes to finest within a rounding, about 6e-8 at 5e8, far short of the half Round needs; it comes to exactly 5e8 on arm64, where this was run, so N is finest as the guard says
maths/hexes/sphere.go Nearest: cands := [3][2]int{{i0, j0}, {i0 + 1, j0}, {i0, j0 + 1}} => cands := [3][2]int{{i0, j0}, {i0 - 1, j0}, {i0, j0 + 1}} :: the candidates choose where walk starts, and walk goes on to the nearest corner of the face from any start within three steps, which the walk test holds against every corner of the face; a changed candidate is at most two steps from the nearest, so the tile is the same and only the work differs. this rests on that test and the brute-force tests, not on a proof
maths/hexes/sphere.go Nearest: cands := [3][2]int{{i0, j0}, {i0 + 1, j0}, {i0, j0 + 1}} => cands := [3][2]int{{i0, j0}, {i0 + 2, j0}, {i0, j0 + 1}} :: the candidates choose where walk starts, and walk goes on to the nearest corner of the face from any start within three steps, which the walk test holds against every corner of the face; a changed candidate is at most two steps from the nearest, so the tile is the same and only the work differs. this rests on that test and the brute-force tests, not on a proof
