maths/hexes/sphere.go Nearest: best, bi, bj := math.Inf(1), 0, 0 => best, bi, bj := math.Inf(2), 0, 0 :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
maths/hexes/sphere.go Nearest: best, bi, bj := math.Inf(1), 0, 0 => best, bi, bj := math.Inf(1), 1, 0 :: the start is replaced by the first candidate, whose distance is a number below endless, unless p has no number in it; the package takes a direction, and one with no number in it has no tile
maths/hexes/sphere.go Nearest: best, bi, bj := math.Inf(1), 0, 0 => best, bi, bj := math.Inf(1), 0, 1 :: the start is replaced by the first candidate, whose distance is a number below endless, unless p has no number in it; the package takes a direction, and one with no number in it has no tile
maths/hexes/sphere.go Nearest: d < best => d <= best :: a tie is two corners exactly as near the point; the package promises the nearest, and either of two equally near is it
maths/hexes/sphere.go walk: d < best => d <= best :: a tie is two corners exactly as near the point; the package promises the nearest, and either of two equally near is it
maths/hexes/sphere.go walk: if !moved { break } => if !moved { } :: when nothing moved, i and j are as they were, so the rounds that follow repeat the first and end the same way, only later
maths/hexes/sphere.go settle: if t.inside(tile) { return tile } => if t.inside(tile) { } :: settle is given the tile walk ended on, whose neighbours inside the face walk has weighed, and an inside tile has no neighbour off its face; the look across faces from an edge tile is held by the settle test. this rests on reading, not on a run
maths/hexes/sphere.go settle: d < best => d <= best :: a tie is two corners exactly as near the point; the package promises the nearest, and either of two equally near is it
maths/hexes/sphere.go clamp: i+j > n => i+j >= n :: when i+j is n, j is already n-i, and the lines that follow set j to n-i and i to n-j, which they are: nothing moves
maths/hexes/sphere.go inside: return tile.I > 0 && tile.J > 0 && tile.I+tile.J < t.n() => return tile.I > 1 && tile.J > 0 && tile.I+tile.J < t.n() :: a tile at 1 called an edge tile is looked for on the other faces by canonical and near and found on none, and settle finds none nearer, as for any tile walk ended on; the canonical test holds the first for every corner of every face at several splits and for a sample at the finest. the tile and its neighbours are the same, only the work is not
maths/hexes/sphere.go inside: return tile.I > 0 && tile.J > 0 && tile.I+tile.J < t.n() => return tile.I > 0 && tile.J > 1 && tile.I+tile.J < t.n() :: a tile at 1 called an edge tile is looked for on the other faces by canonical and near and found on none, and settle finds none nearer, as for any tile walk ended on; the canonical test holds the first for every corner of every face at several splits and for a sample at the finest. the tile and its neighbours are the same, only the work is not
maths/hexes/sphere.go canonical: if t.inside(tile) { return tile } => if t.inside(tile) { } :: a corner inside a face is on no other face, so the loop over the other faces finds none and the tile comes back as it was; the canonical test holds that for every corner of every face at several splits and for a sample at the finest
maths/hexes/sphere.go canonical: g < f => g <= f :: on(f, q) of a corner q of face f gives back that corner's own i and j, so it returns the tile the loop falls through to
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: q.Dot(normals[g]) < 0.7 => q.Dot(normals[g]) <= 0.7 :: a dot of exactly 0.7 is all it changes; a corner of the face has a dot of 0.7946 or more with its middle, and a direction with a dot of 0.7 that is not is turned away by the weights that follow
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: i and j are read only when ok is true; canonical and near, the only callers, test ok first
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: i and j are read only when ok is true; canonical and near, the only callers, test ok first
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: u < -slack || v < -slack || w < -slack => u <= -slack || v < -slack || w < -slack :: this changes a threshold only at the one value it sits on, a weight of exactly -1e-9 or a distance from a whole number of exactly 1e-6 as a double; they are allowances for rounding, not promises, and the on test holds a value each side of each
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: u < -slack || v < -slack || w < -slack => u < -slack || v <= -slack || w < -slack :: this changes a threshold only at the one value it sits on, a weight of exactly -1e-9 or a distance from a whole number of exactly 1e-6 as a double; they are allowances for rounding, not promises, and the on test holds a value each side of each
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: u < -slack || v < -slack || w < -slack => u < -slack || v < -slack || w <= -slack :: this changes a threshold only at the one value it sits on, a weight of exactly -1e-9 or a distance from a whole number of exactly 1e-6 as a double; they are allowances for rounding, not promises, and the on test holds a value each side of each
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: i and j are read only when ok is true; canonical and near, the only callers, test ok first
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: i and j are read only when ok is true; canonical and near, the only callers, test ok first
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: math.Abs(fi-float64(i)) > 1e-6 || math.Abs(fj-float64(j)) > 1e-6 => math.Abs(fi-float64(i)) >= 1e-6 || math.Abs(fj-float64(j)) > 1e-6 :: this changes a threshold only at the one value it sits on, a weight of exactly -1e-9 or a distance from a whole number of exactly 1e-6 as a double; they are allowances for rounding, not promises, and the on test holds a value each side of each
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: math.Abs(fi-float64(i)) > 1e-6 || math.Abs(fj-float64(j)) > 1e-6 => math.Abs(fi-float64(i)) > 1e-6 || math.Abs(fj-float64(j)) >= 1e-6 :: this changes a threshold only at the one value it sits on, a weight of exactly -1e-9 or a distance from a whole number of exactly 1e-6 as a double; they are allowances for rounding, not promises, and the on test holds a value each side of each
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: return 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, false :: i and j are read only when ok is true; canonical and near, the only callers, test ok first
maths/hexes/sphere.go on: return 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, false :: i and j are read only when ok is true; canonical and near, the only callers, test ok first
maths/hexes/sphere.go near: k < len(buf) => k <= len(buf) :: the guard fires only for a seventh distinct neighbour, which no tile has: five at the twelve pentagons and six elsewhere, as the tests count for every tile; past it the change would index off the end, not differ
maths/hexes/sphere.go near: if t.inside(tile) { return k } => if t.inside(tile) { } :: a corner inside a face is on no other face, so the loop over the other faces finds none and the tile comes back as it was; the canonical test holds that for every corner of every face at several splits and for a sample at the finest
maths/hexes/sphere.go near: if g == tile.Face { continue } => if g == tile.Face { } :: on(face, middle) is the tile itself, whose steps are the ones already added, and add refuses a tile it has
maths/hexes/sphere.go Neighbours: return t.ID(out[a]) < t.ID(out[b]) => return t.ID(out[a]) <= t.ID(out[b]) :: the lists are at most six long, which sort.Slice orders by insertion, asking only about two different tiles, whose ids differ, so <= orders as <
maths/hexes/sphere.go Edge: best := math.Inf(1) => best := math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
maths/hexes/sphere.go Tiles: i++ => i-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
maths/hexes/sphere.go Tiles: i+j <= n => i-j <= n :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
maths/hexes/sphere.go Tiles: j++ => j-- :: a loop that never ends, named because the run can only time it out
maths/hexes/sphere.go face: best, at := -2.0, 0 => best, at := -2.0, 1 :: the first normal's dot is never below -1, so at is set on the first round unless the dot is no number, which only a direction with no number in it makes
maths/hexes/sphere.go face: d > best => d >= best :: a direction as near one face's middle as another's is on the edge between them, which both faces hold, and the tile found from either is the one on that edge. this rests on reading and the edge tests, not on a proof
maths/space.go Hit: if !(enter <= leave) { return 0, 0, false } => if !(enter <= leave) { } :: through the loop enter only rises and leave only falls, by Max and Min, and a NaN in either stays NaN, so the check after the loop refuses the same rays with the same nought, nought, false; this one only stops sooner
maths/space.go Hit: !(enter <= leave) || math.IsInf(enter, 1) => !(enter <= leave) || math.IsInf(enter, 2) :: math.IsInf tests for positive infinity for any sign above nought, so 2 is 1
render/colour/colour.go bloomed: sum += weights[i] => sum -= weights[i] :: the sum comes out as exactly minus itself, so every weight changes sign exactly; the across pass and the down pass both use the weights, so the two signs cancel and the spill is the same to the bit
render/colour/colour.go squeeze: math.IsInf(y, 1) => math.IsInf(y, 2) :: math.IsInf tests for positive infinity for any sign above nought, so 2 is 1
render/colour/colour.go Encode: if math.IsNaN(v) { return 0 } => if math.IsNaN(v) { } :: holds on arm64 only, and rests on the run: there a NaN converted to an integer is nought, which is the byte the check returns; the check is for machines that convert it to something else, and none is here to show it
render/colour/false.go Stands: y > 0 => y >= 0 :: y is nought only for a colour with no light in any lamp, and the colours a range lands on are the observer's whole-nanometre rows, each of which has some, as TestEveryRowTheRangesLandOnHasLight checks; so the scale by 1 over y is never met with a y of nought
render/colour/gauge.go Masked: aspect > have => aspect >= have :: when aspect is have the whole picture is kept: the rows kept are round(h * have / aspect), which is h, so no row is outside them and nothing is cut, as when neither case is taken
render/colour/gauge.go Masked: aspect < have => aspect <= have :: when aspect is have the whole picture is kept: the columns kept are round(w * aspect / have), which is w, so no column is outside them and nothing is cut, as when neither case is taken
render/colour/gauge.go Masked: y < h => y <= h :: the extra pass is at row h, one past the picture, and SetRGBA ignores a point outside the bounds of the copy, which are the picture's own
render/colour/gauge.go Masked: x < w => x <= w :: the extra pass is at column w, one past the picture, and SetRGBA ignores a point outside the bounds of the copy, which are the picture's own
render/colour/gauge.go Grained: size < 1 => size <= 1 :: at a size of exactly 1 the branch sets the amount to amount * 1 and the size to 1, which they already are
render/colour/gauge.go Grained: y < b.Dy() => y <= b.Dy() :: the extra pass is at row Dy, one past the picture; reading it gives the zero colour, with no panic, and SetRGBA ignores a point outside the bounds of the copy, which are the picture's own
render/colour/gauge.go Grained: x < b.Dx() => x <= b.Dx() :: the extra pass is at column Dx, one past the picture; reading it gives the zero colour, with no panic, and SetRGBA ignores a point outside the bounds of the copy, which are the picture's own
# scene/sky
scene/sky/sky.go Light: up = maths.V(0, 1, 0) => up = maths.V(0, 2, 0) :: the up is only ever read through Unit or direction, and the unit of (0, 2, 0) is exactly (0, 1, 0): its length is 2, one over that is a half, and a half of 2 is 1, none of it rounded; so the up read is the same
scene/sky/sky.go direction: if s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) { nan := math.NaN() return maths.V(nan, nan, nan) } => if s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) { } :: the guard returns early what the division under it reaches anyway: a largest part of nought divides 0 by 0, an endless one divides itself by itself, and no number divides every part to no number, so each leaves a part that is no number, and Unit of that is no number in every part, as the guard gives. read from the code; the tests of every such way pass either way
scene/sky/sky.go direction: s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) => (s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 0)) && math.IsNaN(s) :: the guard returns early what the division under it reaches anyway: a largest part of nought divides 0 by 0, an endless one divides itself by itself, and no number divides every part to no number, so each leaves a part that is no number, and Unit of that is no number in every part, as the guard gives. read from the code; the tests of every such way pass either way
scene/sky/sky.go direction: s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) => s == 0 && math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) :: the guard returns early what the division under it reaches anyway: a largest part of nought divides 0 by 0, an endless one divides itself by itself, and no number divides every part to no number, so each leaves a part that is no number, and Unit of that is no number in every part, as the guard gives. read from the code; the tests of every such way pass either way
scene/sky/sky.go direction: s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) => s == 1 || math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) :: a largest part of 1 is one Unit can measure, and direction has already returned its unit by the time s is read, so s is never 1 there; and nought is then left to the division, which gives no number as the guard does
scene/sky/sky.go direction: s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 0) || math.IsNaN(s) => s == 0 || math.IsInf(s, 1) || math.IsNaN(s) :: s is the largest of three absolute values, so it is never minus endless, and IsInf(s, 1) is IsInf(s, 0)
scene/sky/stars.go star: apart > radius => apart >= radius :: at apart equal to radius, apart over radius is exactly 1, the smooth step from 0 to 1 is 1 there, the edge is nought, and the star adds its light times nought, which is nought, as the early return adds. read from the code, not shown by a run, and true for a finite Bright and a radius above nought: an endless Bright times nought is no number, and with apart and radius both nought the edge is nought over nought
scene/texture/aurora.go At: h < 0.15 => h <= 0.15 :: at h equal to 0.15 the ease Smooth(0, 0.15, h) is one, so the blend is the fringe times nought plus the bottom times one, which is the bottom the next case returns. read from the code, and true for a finite fringe
scene/texture/aurora.go At: h < 0.35 => h <= 0.35 :: at h equal to 0.35 the ease Smooth(0.35, 1, h) is nought, so the blend is the bottom times one plus the top times nought, which is the bottom the case above returns. read from the code, and true for a finite top
scene/texture/hexed.go Paint: return h.Palette[min(int(k), len(h.Palette)-1)] => return h.Palette[min(int(k), len(h.Palette)+1)] :: the hash is at most 1 - 2^-53, since noise.unit divides 53 bits by 2^53, and that times a palette of n rounds to below n, so int(k) is at most n-1 and the min never takes its second argument. argued for any n, and shown by a run of every n to two million with the largest hash
scene/texture/picture.go At: u := lon/(2*math.Pi) + 0.5 => u := lon/(2*math.Pi) - 0.5 :: the two differ by exactly one, and the next line takes the fraction of u, so u is the same after it but for the last bit of rounding. read from the code, not shown by a run to be exact
scene/texture/picture.go At: u -= math.Floor(u) => u += math.Floor(u) :: the two differ by a whole number, 2*floor(u), so x differs by a whole number of widths, and sample takes its column modulo the width: the same columns with the same share, but for the last bit of rounding. read from the code, not shown by a run to be exact
scene/texture/texture.go At: (cell(p.X)+cell(p.Y)+cell(p.Z))%2 == 0 => (cell(p.X)+cell(p.Y)-cell(p.Z))%2 == 0 :: a sum and a difference of the same integers differ by twice one of them, so they are both even or both odd, and %2 == 0 asks only for even (an odd one is 1 or -1, never 0). it holds when the sum wraps too, since a wrap is by 2^64. true on every machine, read from the code
scene/texture/texture.go At: (cell(p.X)+cell(p.Y)+cell(p.Z))%2 == 0 => (cell(p.X)-cell(p.Y)+cell(p.Z))%2 == 0 :: a sum and a difference of the same integers differ by twice one of them, so they are both even or both odd, and %2 == 0 asks only for even (an odd one is 1 or -1, never 0). it holds when the sum wraps too, since a wrap is by 2^64. true on every machine, read from the code
scene/texture/thin.go profile: r <= 1 => r < 1 :: at r equal to 1 the formula gives Pow(1, 0.75) times Pow(1-Sqrt(1), 0.25), which is one times nought, the nought the early return gives. read from the code, and the test of profile(1) passes under it
scene/texture/thin.go Temperature: t.Inner <= 0 || r < t.Inner || r > t.Outer => t.Inner <= 0 || r <= t.Inner || r > t.Outer :: at r equal to the inner radius profile(1) is nought, so the temperature is Hot times nought over the peak, the nought the early return gives. read from the code, and true for a finite Hot: an endless one times nought is no number
scene/texture/thin.go At: if math.IsNaN(k) { return maths.Grey(math.NaN()) } => if math.IsNaN(k) { } :: holds only on arm64. with k no number thermal takes int(NaN) as its index, which is 0 on arm64, so it gives a light of parts that are no number, as the guard does. on amd64 int(NaN) is the lowest int and the table index panics, which a test would see. shown by a run on arm64 only; the amd64 half is read from how int(NaN) is defined there, not run
scene/texture/thin.go At: t.Streaks > 0 => t.Streaks >= 0 :: at Streaks nought the factor is Max(0, 1 + 0*2*n), which is one for any n, since noise.Fractal gives a number for any input (it stops at a coordinate that is not finite, and Gradient is a number for a finite one). read from noise.go, not shown by a run
scene/texture/thin.go thermal: k <= 0 => k < 0 :: at k nought the lookup is thermals[0] times one plus thermals[1] times nought, which is thermals[0], the entry the early return gives. read from the code, and true while the entries are finite: each is a clipped colour scaled by one over a luminance above nought, or black
scene/texture/banded.go At: b.Jets > 0 => b.Jets >= 0 :: with Jets nought the jet is 0.35 * sin(0 * pi * lat), which is nought for a lat that is a number, so lon+jet is lon; for a lat that is no number the colour is no number whichever. read from the code
scene/texture/banded.go At: b.Roil > 0 => b.Roil >= 0 :: with Roil nought the push is nought times a noise.Fractal that is always a number, so lat is as it was, but for the sign of a zero, which band does not see. read from the code and noise.go
scene/texture/banded.go At: b.Fine > 0 => b.Fine >= 0 :: with Fine nought the push is nought times a noise.Fractal that is always a number, so lat is as it was, but for the sign of a zero, which band does not see. read from the code and noise.go
scene/texture/banded.go At: b.Mottle > 0 => b.Mottle >= 0 :: with Mottle nought the scale is 1 + 0*m for a noise.Fractal m that is always a number, which is one, and Scale(1) is the band. read from the code and noise.go
scene/texture/banded.go At: tintBy > 0 => tintBy >= 0 :: tintBy is nought only when no spot reached the point, or each reached it with a share rounded to nought, and tint is then Light{} (a finite paint times nought, added up), so band times one plus tint times nought is band. read from the code, and true for a finite spot paint: one with a part that is no number times nought is no number
scene/texture/banded.go band: if math.IsNaN(lat) { return maths.Grey(math.NaN()) } => if math.IsNaN(lat) { } :: holds only on arm64. with lat no number, t is none and int(Floor(t)) is 0 on arm64, so the blend of two paints by a share that is no number is a light of parts that are no number, as the guard returns. on amd64 int(NaN) is the lowest int and Paints[i] panics, which a test would see. shown by a run on arm64 only; the amd64 half is read from how int(NaN) is defined there, not run
scene/texture/banded.go band: if n == 1 { return b.Paints[0] } => if n == 1 { } :: with one paint t is a latitude clamped, plus one, over two, times nought, which is nought, so i is 0, i >= n-1 holds, and Paints[n-1] is Paints[0]. read from the code, for a lat that is a number (one that is not returns above)
scene/texture/banded.go band: t := (math.Max(-1, math.Min(1, lat)) + 1) / 2 * float64(n-1) => t := (math.Max(-1, math.Min(2, lat)) + 1) / 2 * float64(n-1) :: a latitude above 1 gives a t above n-1, and i >= n-1 returns the last paint, which is what the clamp to 1 gives, t exactly n-1. read from the code
scene/thing/radiant.go package: return rs[i].Source.Hi-rs[i].Source.Lo < rs[j].Source.Hi-rs[j].Source.Lo => return rs[i].Source.Hi-rs[i].Source.Lo <= rs[j].Source.Hi-rs[j].Source.Lo :: the two answer differently only for two ranges of one width, and no two of the ranges are the same width, which TestNoTwoRangesAreTheSameWidth holds; the sort never compares equals
scene/thing/radiant.go package: return rs[i].Source.Hi-rs[i].Source.Lo < rs[j].Source.Hi-rs[j].Source.Lo => return rs[i].Source.Hi-rs[i].Source.Lo < rs[j].Source.Hi+rs[j].Source.Lo :: the change calls the visible narrower than the ultraviolet (470 under 500), which it is not, but only when the visible is the one being placed next to the ultraviolet, and the insertion sort Go runs on fewer than twenty never places it, as it is first; every comparison the sort does make gives the answer it gives unchanged. traced by hand over the eight ranges, and TestRangesRunNarrowestFirst passed under the change in a run; it holds for these eight ranges and no others
scene/thing/radiant.go Excitation: nearest, apart := 0.0, math.Inf(1) => nearest, apart := 0.0, math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
scene/world/feature.go Shape: share > 1 => share >= 1 :: at a share of exactly 1 the last line is Rise times (1-(1-0.2)/0.8) to the 1.6, and (1-0.2)/0.8 is exactly 1 in floating point (a run of it said so), so the power is 0 and the height comes back either way; only a Rise of infinity makes a number that is none there, which nothing promises. read from the code, with that one run
scene/world/feature.go carve: b > Unfixed => b >= Unfixed :: the test sits inside the one that b is not Unfixed, so b above Unfixed and b at or above it are the same set. read from the code
scene/world/fine.go Paint: f.vary > 0 => f.vary >= 0 :: at a vary of nought the scale is 1 + 0*h for a hash h within a half of nought, which is exactly 1, and scaling a paint by 1 gives the paint. read from the code
scene/world/world.go index: return -1 => return -2 :: the only two callers, Biome and Height, ask for i >= 0, so any negative number says the same. read from the code
scene/world/world.go plates: land[p] = noise.Hash(seed+4, k) < 0.42 => land[p] = noise.Hash(seed+4, k) <= 0.42 :: a hash is a whole number over 2^53 and the double nearest 0.42 is 7566047373982433 over 2^54, an odd number, so no hash is equal to 0.42 and < and <= agree on every hash. the odd number is from a run; the rest is read from noise.unit
scene/world/world.go plates: best := math.Inf(1) => best := math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
scene/world/world.go boundaries: if pi == pj { continue } => if pi == pj { } :: two tiles of one plate have one drift, so rel is a product taken from itself, exactly nought, and closing and the effect with it; an effect of nought never beats the strict compare against a push of nought or more. read from the code, for a drift that is a number, which plates draws
scene/world/world.go meeting: closing > 0 => closing >= 0 :: at a closing of exactly nought every branch gives its factor times nought, which is nought but for a sign that boundaries does not read (it asks != 0 and Abs), so the branch taken does not matter. read from the code
scene/world/world.go meeting: closing > 0 => closing >= 0 :: at a closing of exactly nought every branch gives its factor times nought, which is nought but for a sign that boundaries does not read (it asks != 0 and Abs), so the branch taken does not matter. read from the code
scene/world/world.go meeting: closing > 0 => closing >= 0 :: at a closing of exactly nought every branch gives its factor times nought, which is nought but for a sign that boundaries does not read (it asks != 0 and Abs), so the branch taken does not matter. read from the code
scene/world/world.go meeting: closing > 0 => closing >= 0 :: at a closing of exactly nought every branch gives its factor times nought, which is nought but for a sign that boundaries does not read (it asks != 0 and Abs), so the branch taken does not matter. read from the code
scene/world/world.go spread: reach[i] = -1 => reach[i] = -2 :: reach below nought only means not yet reached: it is read as >= 0, and reach+1 is taken only from a tile already reached. read from the code
scene/world/world.go pourSea: e > 0 && top > 0 => e >= 0 && top > 0 :: e is nought only for a tile at the water line, and nought over a top above nought is nought, the same as leaving it. read from the code
scene/world/world.go pourSea: e < 0 && bottom > 0 => e <= 0 && bottom > 0 :: e is nought only for a tile at the water line, and nought over a bottom above nought is nought, the same as leaving it. read from the code
scene/world/world.go toSea: d[i] = -1 => d[i] = -2 :: d below nought only means not yet reached: it is read as < 0, and d+1 is taken only from a tile already reached. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go Picture: 6 => 7 :: a seventh square is written at the cell's own left edge and three rows down, which is the top left square of the cell below, drawn later in the same row-by-row walk and written over, or on the last row past the image, where SetRGBA writes nothing. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go Glyph: p < 21 => p <= 21 :: the case p == 21 comes first and takes 21, so no p that reaches this case is 21, and for the rest below 21 and up to 21 are the same. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go Glyph: 21 => 22 :: p < 22 differs from p < 21 only at 21, which the case p == 21 before it has already taken. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go Glyph: p < 42 => p <= 42 :: the case p == 42 comes first and takes 42, so no p that reaches this case is 42, and for the rest below 42 and up to 42 are the same. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go Glyph: 42 => 43 :: p < 43 differs from p < 42 only at 42, which the case p == 42 before it has already taken. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go split: best, bestErr := Cell{}, math.Inf(1) => best, bestErr := Cell{}, math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
interfaces/terminal/cells.go split: p < 64 => p <= 64 :: pattern 64 has none of the six bits set, so it is pattern 0 again with the same error, and a pattern replaces the best only by being strictly less. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go split: p < 64 => p < 65 :: the patterns go up by two from nought, so below 65 is up to 64, which is pattern 0 again with the same error, and a pattern replaces the best only by being strictly less. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/cells.go mean: if n == 0 { return color.RGBA{A: 255} } => if n == 0 { } :: with none, k is endless and each sum is nought, nought times endless is no number, and Encode draws a part that is no number as nought, its doc says, which is the black the return gives, with the same alpha. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/terminal.go Measure: ws.Xpixel > 0 && ws.Ypixel > 0 && ws.Col > 0 && ws.Row > 0 => ws.Xpixel > 0 && ws.Ypixel > 0 && ws.Col >= 0 && ws.Row > 0 :: this line is reached only after the check above has returned for a Col of nought, and Col is unsigned, so above nought and not below nought are both true here. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/terminal.go Measure: ws.Xpixel > 0 && ws.Ypixel > 0 && ws.Col > 0 && ws.Row > 0 => ws.Xpixel > 0 && ws.Ypixel > 0 && ws.Col > 0 && ws.Row >= 0 :: this line is reached only after the check above has returned for a Row of nought, and Row is unsigned, so above nought and not below nought are both true here. read from the code
interfaces/terminal/terminal.go Open: if err != nil { return nil, err } => if err != nil { } :: MakeRaw reads the modes of a descriptor IsTerminal has just read the same way, then writes them back, and I found no terminal that answers the first and refuses the second: a pseudo-terminal whose master end was closed still took both in a run. no test here makes MakeRaw fail, so the line is not reached. read from the code and one run
interfaces/terminal/terminal.go Next: err != nil || n == 0 => err != nil && n == 0 :: an os.File read into 16 bytes gives n of nought with an error, an end of file for a read of nothing, and n above nought with none, as poll.FD.Read zeroes n on any error and eofError makes an empty read an error, so the two halves are both true or both false. read from the Go 1.27.0 source
scene/gas/gas.go optics: return math.Inf(1), 0 => return math.Inf(2), 0 :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1). read from go doc math.Inf, which says positive infinity for a sign of nought or more
scene/bodies/bodies.go build: back := tail.Trail.Scale(-1) => back := tail.Trail.Scale(-2) :: back is projected off the way away and made a unit vector in the next two lines, and 2 is a power of two, so the doubled trail doubles every product, sum and square after it exactly in binary and the unit vector comes out the same bit for bit. read from the code, and run once as a scratch copy of the lines on 2,000,000 random trails, 200,000 of them along the way away, with none differing; it holds short of the ends of the float range, where Len stops measuring (parts near 1e154 or 1e-156)
scene/bodies/bodies.go sound: !(scale > 0) || math.IsInf(scale, 1) => !(scale > 0) || math.IsInf(scale, 2) :: math.IsInf with any positive sign asks after positive infinity (go doc math.IsInf: if sign > 0, IsInf reports whether f is positive infinity), so 2 is 1
scene/bodies/bodies.go orbitable: !(years < 292) => !(years <= 292) :: years is a float from a root and a division, so it is exactly 292 at a single mass and nowhere near it otherwise, and there the orbit is inside what a Duration holds (292.27 years, MaxInt64 nanoseconds over 31,557,600 seconds) so thing.Period takes it either way; refusing 292 exactly or taking it makes no difference to any orbit the package is asked about. read from the code and the arithmetic, not run
scene/bodies/template/venus.go Venus: if !ok { return nil, fmt.Errorf("no land for maat") } => if !ok { } :: this branch is not reached. Find walks outward from the nearest tile over every neighbour and fails only when no tile it can reach is land; maat's Find asks the same world for the same class as maxwell's, which found a tile or has already returned its own refusal, so a land tile exists and maat's finds one too. read from world.Find, and run: TestFindOnASphereFailsOnlyWhereThereIsNoLand finds land from 300 directions in venus's world at 4 splits and from none in a drowned one

# render/trace
render/trace/field.go package: bends = 40000 => bends = 40001 :: a march takes anything near 40000 steps only if a ray stays on the photon sphere that long; bisecting the shadow's edge to the last bit from outside the reach, and from still eyes at 3, 4 and 8 masses out, 320 rays that sit as near the edge as doubles allow, the longest march took 2,565 steps, so no ray reaches a 40001st place to be given or refused. rests on that run, made as a scratch test and deleted, and on this arithmetic
render/trace/field.go entering: if disc < 0 { continue } => if disc < 0 { } :: below nought disc has no root, so t is no number, which passes neither t <= 1e-9 nor d < at, and no field is chosen with the skip or without it
render/trace/field.go entering: t <= 1e-9 => t < 1e-9 :: they differ only for a t of exactly the double 1e-9. t is minus b less a root, so it is at most the size of b; for a b of 2e-9 or more it is never that double, an odd multiple of 2^-82, since a difference of doubles that size is a multiple of 2^-81; for a smaller b the start is on the reach to within b squared over twice the reach, with the ray tangent to it. the nearest place to build one, a field of no reach with the ray starting 1e-9 before it, is in TestEnteringKnowsTheEdgeOfTheReach, and it does not land: on arm64, where b*b - c is fused, disc comes out 5.3e-35 and not nought, so t is 9.99999993e-10, under 1e-9, and < and <= agree. read from the code, and run once as a scratch copy of the lines; holds on arm64 only
render/trace/field.go gain: !(g >= 0) => !(g > 0) :: they differ only at g of exactly nought, where the early return gives 0 and the line after it gives the lesser of 50 and nought to the fourth, which is 0
render/trace/field.go bent: enter > 0 => enter >= 0 :: an enter of nought is an eye inside the reach, and through, given a far of nought, crosses nothing, since Box.Hit and every Span in scene/medium clip to the far they are given (read from each), so it returns a keep of one and no light, which m.keep and m.front already are
render/trace/field.go bent: i < bends && shows(m.keep) => i <= bends && shows(m.keep) :: it allows one step more than bends, and no ray takes a number of steps near bends: see the first line of this section
render/trace/field.go march: if length <= 0 { return frame.Pixel{}, false } => if length <= 0 { } :: a step of no length has a direction of nought times one over nought, no number, which meets nothing in Sphere.Hit, Plane.Hit, Box.Hit or a marched shape, no number passing any of their comparisons, and over a far of nought nothing is gathered, so m.inside, m.keep and m.depth are left as the early return leaves them; m.shift.p is set, and the next step sets it before it reads it. a mesh's Hit was not read. read from the code; no step in a test here is of no length
render/trace/field.go march: length <= 0 => length < 0 :: length is a Len and never under nought, so < differs from <= only at nought, the case on the line above
render/trace/field.go leave: left > 0 => left >= 0 :: a left of exactly nought is a leg of no length: throughGain over a far of nought gathers nothing (Box.Hit and every Span in scene/medium clip to far), keep and inside are left as they are, and exit.At moves by nought; m.shift.p is set, which nothing reads after leave. read from the code
render/trace/trace.go placeVolumes: g.PhaseAt(1) => g.PhaseAt(2) :: the call is made for its refusal and its value is thrown away; PhaseAt panics on the asymmetry alone and never on the cosine (read from gas.PhaseAt), so 2 is refused when 1 is
render/trace/trace.go crossings: len(cs) > 1 => len(cs) >= 1 :: sorting a single crossing moves nothing
render/trace/trace.go gasClear: k := 2 / step => k := 3 / step :: k only sets how many wobbles of the jitter fall in a step, 2 here and 3 in the change; at either the jitter stays near nought to one of a step, so each sample lies in its own step, which TestEverySampleOfAShadowLiesInItsOwnStep holds, and points side by side agree, which TestTheJitterOfAShadowIsSmoothInSpace holds, and both pass with the change. how many wobbles is a choice of dither and not a promise, and a test of it would hold the noise's own numbers
render/trace/trace.go gasClear: jitter := 0.5 + 0.5*noise.Gradient(77, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) => jitter := 0.5 - 0.5*noise.Gradient(77, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) :: a half less half the noise is as good a dither as a half plus half: it stays near nought to one of a step and is as smooth, and TestEverySampleOfAShadowLiesInItsOwnStep and TestTheJitterOfAShadowIsSmoothInSpace both pass with the change. which sign is drawn is a choice of dither and not a promise, and a test of it would hold the noise's own numbers
render/trace/trace.go gasClear: jitter := 0.5 + 0.5*noise.Gradient(77, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) => jitter := 0.5 + 0.5*noise.Gradient(78, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) :: another seed is another dither, as good, and TestEverySampleOfAShadowLiesInItsOwnStep and TestTheJitterOfAShadowIsSmoothInSpace both pass with it. which seed is drawn is a choice and not a promise, and a test of it would hold the noise's own numbers
render/trace/trace.go gasClear: if !any { return maths.Grey(1) } => if !any { } :: with no gas crossed depth is the zero light, and math.Exp of minus nought is one, so RGB(1, 1, 1) is Grey(1) to the bit
render/trace/trace.go stepping: s.next = s.buf[:0] => s.next = s.buf[:1] :: next is read only at the indices of the crossings, below len(cs), and all of them are nought at the start whether or not there is one more at the front
render/trace/trace.go take: k = -1 => k = -2 :: k is only asked whether it is under nought, and is set again before it is given back
render/trace/trace.go take: return 0, 0, 0, false => return 1, 0, 0, false :: take's other results are not read when ok is false: both callers, throughGain and throughIn, stop on it
render/trace/trace.go take: return 0, 0, 0, false => return 0, 1, 0, false :: take's other results are not read when ok is false: both callers, throughGain and throughIn, stop on it
render/trace/trace.go take: return 0, 0, 0, false => return 0, 0, 1, false :: take's other results are not read when ok is false: both callers, throughGain and throughIn, stop on it
render/trace/trace.go clear: k := 2 / step => k := 3 / step :: k only sets how many wobbles of the jitter fall in a step, 2 here and 3 in the change; at either the jitter stays near nought to one of a step, so each sample lies in its own step, which TestEverySampleOfAShadowLiesInItsOwnStep holds, and points side by side agree, which TestTheJitterOfAShadowIsSmoothInSpace holds, and both pass with the change. how many wobbles is a choice of dither and not a promise, and a test of it would hold the noise's own numbers
render/trace/trace.go clear: jitter := 0.5 + 0.5*noise.Gradient(77, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) => jitter := 0.5 - 0.5*noise.Gradient(77, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) :: a half less half the noise is as good a dither as a half plus half: it stays near nought to one of a step and is as smooth, and TestEverySampleOfAShadowLiesInItsOwnStep and TestTheJitterOfAShadowIsSmoothInSpace both pass with the change. which sign is drawn is a choice of dither and not a promise, and a test of it would hold the noise's own numbers
render/trace/trace.go clear: jitter := 0.5 + 0.5*noise.Gradient(77, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) => jitter := 0.5 + 0.5*noise.Gradient(78, world.X*k, world.Y*k, world.Z*k) :: another seed is another dither, as good, and TestEverySampleOfAShadowLiesInItsOwnStep and TestTheJitterOfAShadowIsSmoothInSpace both pass with it. which seed is drawn is a choice and not a promise, and a test of it would hold the noise's own numbers
render/trace/trace.go meet: n, f := near/p.scale, far/p.scale => n, f := near*p.scale, far/p.scale :: near is nought at every call, first and blocked both passing nought, and nought times a scale or over one is nought
render/trace/trace.go march: return 0, false => return 1, false :: meet's callers, first and blocked, ask ok before they read the distance
render/trace/trace.go march: return 0, false => return 1, false :: meet's callers, first and blocked, ask ok before they read the distance
render/trace/trace.go shade: if len(sc.vs) == 0 { return px } => if len(sc.vs) == 0 { } :: through of no volumes is a keep of one and no light, and the line after returns px for just that
render/trace/trace.go surface: l := air(sc.w, r.Dir, math.Inf(1), sky(sc.w, r.Dir)) => l := air(sc.w, r.Dir, math.Inf(2), sky(sc.w, r.Dir)) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
render/trace/trace.go surface: return pixelOf(l, 0, float32(math.Inf(1))) => return pixelOf(l, 0, float32(math.Inf(2))) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
render/trace/trace.go surfaceAt: seen := shade(sc, maths.Ray{At: from, Dir: mirrored}, pixel, bounce+1) => seen := shade(sc, maths.Ray{At: from, Dir: mirrored}, pixel, bounce+2) :: bounces is 1, and a ray at bounce 1 or more is not mirrored again, so bounce+2 and bounce+1 both stop it; they would part if bounces were raised past 2
render/trace/trace.go Render: max(1, runtime.NumCPU()) => max(2, runtime.NumCPU()) :: two workers on a machine of one core, and the same on any other; each row is taken by whichever worker is free and a pixel depends on the world alone, so the frame is the same however the rows are shared out, as Render's comment says
render/trace/trace.go Sampled: p.Depth = float32(math.Inf(1)) => p.Depth = float32(math.Inf(2)) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
render/trace/trace.go RenderIn: max(1, runtime.NumCPU()) => max(2, runtime.NumCPU()) :: two workers on a machine of one core, and the same on any other; each row is taken by whichever worker is free and a pixel depends on the world alone, so the frame is the same however the rows are shared out, as Render's comment says and RenderIn shares its rows the same way
render/trace/trace.go inBand: far := math.Inf(1) => far := math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)

render/shortcut/baked.go Density: i >= n-1 => i >= n-2 :: the clamp sets i to n-2, so the one value the change adds, i of n-2, is set to what it already is; every other value acts as before
render/shortcut/baked.go Density: j >= n-1 => j >= n-2 :: the clamp sets j to n-2, so the one value the change adds, j of n-2, is set to what it already is; every other value acts as before
render/shortcut/baked.go Density: k >= n-1 => k >= n-2 :: the clamp sets k to n-2, so the one value the change adds, k of n-2, is set to what it already is; every other value acts as before
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: f := frame.New(1, 1) => f := frame.New(2, 1) :: only the pixel at 0,0 is set and read, the other is black, and a black pixel spills nothing into a neighbour's bloom (bloomed in render/colour/colour.go reads only light above white); a scratch run, since deleted, found the same bytes at 0,0 in 1 by 1, 2 by 1, 1 by 2 and 3 by 3 frames for eight tones and lights from 1e-7 to 1e4
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: f := frame.New(1, 1) => f := frame.New(1, 2) :: only the pixel at 0,0 is set and read, the other is black, and a black pixel spills nothing into a neighbour's bloom (bloomed in render/colour/colour.go reads only light above white); a scratch run, since deleted, found the same bytes at 0,0 in 1 by 1, 2 by 1, 1 by 2 and 3 by 3 frames for eight tones and lights from 1e-7 to 1e4
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: f.Set(0, 0, frame.Pixel{R: float32(l), G: float32(l), B: float32(l), Cover: 1}) => f.Set(0, 0, frame.Pixel{R: float32(l), G: float32(l), B: float32(l), Cover: 2}) :: Develop reads a pixel only through Pixel.Light (render/frame/frame.go), which is R, G and B; Cover is not read
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: return c.R > 0 || c.G > 0 || c.B > 0 => return (c.R > 0 || c.G > 0) && c.B > 0 :: the pixel is grey and Tone.pixel encodes each channel by the same Encode of the same light, so its three bytes are equal and the three tests agree; a scratch run, since deleted, found no pixel with unequal channels for eight tones and lights from 1e-7 to 1e4
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: return c.R > 0 || c.G > 0 || c.B > 0 => return c.R > 0 && c.G > 0 || c.B > 0 :: the pixel is grey and Tone.pixel encodes each channel by the same Encode of the same light, so its three bytes are equal and the three tests agree; a scratch run, since deleted, found no pixel with unequal channels for eight tones and lights from 1e-7 to 1e4
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: return c.R > 0 || c.G > 0 || c.B > 0 => return c.R > 1 || c.G > 0 || c.B > 0 :: the pixel is grey and Tone.pixel encodes each channel by the same Encode of the same light, so its three bytes are equal and the other two tests still say what the first did; a scratch run, since deleted, found no pixel with unequal channels for eight tones and lights from 1e-7 to 1e4
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: return c.R > 0 || c.G > 0 || c.B > 0 => return c.R > 0 || c.G > 1 || c.B > 0 :: the pixel is grey and Tone.pixel encodes each channel by the same Encode of the same light, so its three bytes are equal and the other two tests still say what the second did; a scratch run, since deleted, found no pixel with unequal channels for eight tones and lights from 1e-7 to 1e4
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Threshold: return c.R > 0 || c.G > 0 || c.B > 0 => return c.R > 0 || c.G > 0 || c.B > 1 :: the pixel is grey and Tone.pixel encodes each channel by the same Encode of the same light, so its three bytes are equal and the other two tests still say what the third did; a scratch run, since deleted, found no pixel with unequal channels for eight tones and lights from 1e-7 to 1e4
render/shortcut/cutoff.go Brightest: c > top => c >= top :: when c equals top, top is set to c, which it already is
render/shortcut/error.go Apart: d > worst => d >= worst :: when d equals worst, worst is set to d, which it already is
render/shortcut/error.go Apart: n > 0 => n > 1 :: the two differ only when n is 1, where the mean is divided by one, which changes nothing
render/shortcut/error.go step: a > b => a >= b :: when a equals b the swap puts each where it was

# typography: changes that make no difference to the letters it makes.
typography/font.go Index: return 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) => return 1, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/font.go Kern: return 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning glyphs %d and %d of %d", ErrType, left, right, n) => return 1, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning glyphs %d and %d of %d", ErrType, left, right, n) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, err => return 1, 0, 0, err :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, err => return 0, 1, 0, err :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, err => return 0, 0, 1, err :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) => return 1, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) => return 0, 1, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) => return 0, 0, 1, fmt.Errorf("%w: kerning %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) => return 1, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) => return 0, 1, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/layout.go measured: return 0, 0, 0, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) => return 0, 0, 1, fmt.Errorf("%w: %q: %w", ErrType, letter, err) :: a value returned beside an error is never read: every caller returns on the error and drops the rest
typography/text.go sane: return !math.IsNaN(x) && !math.IsInf(x, 0) => return !math.IsNaN(x) && !math.IsInf(x, 1) :: every number sane checks is also refused below nought, so minus endless is refused either way
typography/plane.go holds: b := r.at(i + 1) => b := r.at(i - 1) :: the ring's steps walked backward are the same steps, crossed as often
typography/plane.go holds: (x > p.x) => (!(x > p.x)) :: a ray toward minus x crosses a closed ring an odd number of times exactly when one toward plus x does
typography/plane.go holds: x > p.x => x <= p.x :: a ray toward minus x crosses a closed ring an odd number of times exactly when one toward plus x does
typography/plane.go holds: x > p.x => x >= p.x :: it differs only for a point on the ring, which holds says may come out either way
typography/plane.go touch: one*two < 0 && three*four < 0 => one*two <= 0 && three*four < 0 :: side is minus one, nought or one: where a product is nought an end lies on the other's line, and the steps then meet at that end, which the line after finds; so less than nought, at most nought, under one and a quotient agree
typography/plane.go touch: one*two < 0 && three*four < 0 => one/two < 0 && three*four < 0 :: side is minus one, nought or one: where a product is nought an end lies on the other's line, and the steps then meet at that end, which the line after finds; so less than nought, at most nought, under one and a quotient agree
typography/plane.go touch: one*two < 0 && three*four < 0 => one*two < 1 && three*four < 0 :: side is minus one, nought or one: where a product is nought an end lies on the other's line, and the steps then meet at that end, which the line after finds; so less than nought, at most nought, under one and a quotient agree
typography/plane.go touch: one*two < 0 && three*four < 0 => one*two < 0 && three*four <= 0 :: side is minus one, nought or one: where a product is nought an end lies on the other's line, and the steps then meet at that end, which the line after finds; so less than nought, at most nought, under one and a quotient agree
typography/plane.go touch: one*two < 0 && three*four < 0 => one*two < 0 && three/four < 0 :: side is minus one, nought or one: where a product is nought an end lies on the other's line, and the steps then meet at that end, which the line after finds; so less than nought, at most nought, under one and a quotient agree
typography/plane.go touch: one*two < 0 && three*four < 0 => one*two < 0 && three*four < 1 :: side is minus one, nought or one: where a product is nought an end lies on the other's line, and the steps then meet at that end, which the line after finds; so less than nought, at most nought, under one and a quotient agree
typography/plane.go side: return 1 => return 2 :: touch asks only a side's sign and whether it is nought
typography/plane.go side: return -1 => return -2 :: touch asks only a side's sign and whether it is nought
typography/rings.go cleaned: i-- => i++ :: a point skipped after a removal is asked on the next pass, which repeats until nothing changes
typography/rings.go idle: if in == (point{}) || out == (point{}) { return true } => if in == (point{}) || out == (point{}) { } :: a step of no length crosses to nought, which is at most nought: idle either way
typography/rings.go idle: in == (point{}) || out == (point{}) => in == (point{}) && out == (point{}) :: a step of no length crosses to nought, which is at most nought: idle either way
typography/rings.go nested: outer := turned(rings[i], 1) => outer := turned(rings[i], 2) :: turned asks only the way's sign
typography/rings.go nested: in.holes = append(in.holes, turned(rings[i], -1)) => in.holes = append(in.holes, turned(rings[i], -2)) :: turned asks only the way's sign
typography/rings.go holders: parent[i] = -1 => parent[i] = -2 :: a parent is asked only whether it is below nought
typography/rings.go holders: i == j || !rings[j].holds(rings[i][0]) => i == j || !rings[j].holds(rings[i][1]) :: rings that do not cross, which crossed has made sure of, hold every point of a ring inside them or none
typography/rings.go holders: parent[i] < 0 || math.Abs(rings[j].area()) < math.Abs(rings[parent[i]].area()) => parent[i] < 0 || math.Abs(rings[j].area()) <= math.Abs(rings[parent[i]].area()) :: two rings that both hold a third and do not cross are one inside the other, so never of equal area
typography/rings.go turned: r.area()*way < 0 => r.area()*way <= 0 :: a cleaned ring that crosses nothing has an area
typography/rings.go turned: r.area()*way < 0 => r.area()/way < 0 :: way is one or minus one
typography/rings.go crossed: steps = append(steps, step{of, at, a, r.at(at + 1)}) => steps = append(steps, step{of, at, a, r.at(at - 1)}) :: the ring's steps walked backward are the same steps, and a step's neighbours are still the places beside it
typography/ears.go bridged: return holes[a][rightmost(holes[a])].x > holes[b][rightmost(holes[b])].x => return holes[a][rightmost(holes[a])].x >= holes[b][rightmost(holes[b])].x :: holes equally far right may be bridged in either order
typography/ears.go rightmost: best := 0 => best := 1 :: the loop asks every point, the first too
typography/ears.go rightmost: p.x > r[best].x => p.x >= r[best].x :: any of the points furthest right sees the outer
typography/ears.go bridge: joined := make(ring, 0, len(made)+len(hole)+2) => joined := make(ring, 0, len(made)+len(hole)-2) :: a capacity only; a ring and a hole each have three points or more
typography/ears.go bridge: joined := make(ring, 0, len(made)+len(hole)+2) => joined := make(ring, 0, len(made)+len(hole)+3) :: a capacity only
typography/ears.go seen: step, along := -1, math.Inf(1) => step, along := -2, math.Inf(1) :: a step is asked only whether it is below nought
typography/ears.go seen: step, along := -1, math.Inf(1) => step, along := -1, math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
typography/ears.go inward: best, angle, near := end, math.Inf(1), math.Inf(1) => best, angle, near := end, math.Inf(2), math.Inf(1) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
typography/ears.go inward: best, angle, near := end, math.Inf(1), math.Inf(1) => best, angle, near := end, math.Inf(1), math.Inf(2) :: math.Inf takes the sign of its argument and any sign above nought is positive, so Inf(2) is Inf(1)
typography/ears.go seen: x >= m.x && x < along => x > m.x && x < along :: a step met at m's own place would put m on the outer, which crossed refuses
typography/ears.go seen: x >= m.x && x < along => x >= m.x && x <= along :: two steps met at the same place meet at the corner they share, the same place in the ring
typography/ears.go inward: turn < angle || turn == angle && way.length() < near => turn < angle || turn == angle && way.length() <= near :: a corner as near in angle and in distance is at the same place, and opening picks among places alike
typography/ears.go opens: p.sub(a).cross(c.sub(p)) >= 0 => p.sub(a).cross(c.sub(p)) > 0 :: a straight corner's two lines are one line, so m is left of both or of neither
typography/ears.go clipped: from := 0 => from := 1 :: any ear may be cut first
typography/ears.go clipped: last := corner(r, left, 1) => last := corner(r, left, 2) :: the last three points from the middle or from the end are one triangle, turned alike
typography/ears.go clipped: last[1].sub(last[0]).cross(last[2].sub(last[1])) <= 0 => last[2].sub(last[0]).cross(last[2].sub(last[1])) <= 0 :: both are twice the triangle's signed area
typography/ears.go earAt: return -1 => return -2 :: an ear is asked only whether it is below nought
typography/extrude.go quad: made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[1], p[2]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[0], n[1], n[2]}) => made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[1], p[2]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[1], n[1], n[2]}) :: every quad is given its first two normals alike and its last two alike
typography/extrude.go quad: made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[1], p[2]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[0], n[1], n[2]}) => made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[1], p[2]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[0], n[1], n[3]}) :: every quad is given its first two normals alike and its last two alike
typography/extrude.go quad: made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[2], p[3]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[0], n[2], n[3]}) => made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[2], p[3]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[1], n[2], n[3]}) :: every quad is given its first two normals alike and its last two alike
typography/extrude.go quad: made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[2], p[3]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[0], n[2], n[3]}) => made.triangle([3]maths.Vec{p[0], p[2], p[3]}, [3]maths.Vec{n[0], n[3], n[3]}) :: every quad is given its first two normals alike and its last two alike
typography/extrude.go solid: front, back := maths.V(0, 0, 1), maths.V(0, 0, -1) => front, back := maths.V(0, 0, 2), maths.V(0, 0, -1) :: mesh.New makes every normal one long
typography/extrude.go solid: front, back := maths.V(0, 0, 1), maths.V(0, 0, -1) => front, back := maths.V(0, 0, 1), maths.V(0, 0, -2) :: mesh.New makes every normal one long
typography/extrude.go band: z < 0 => z <= 0 :: z is one or minus one
typography/extrude.go band: z < 0 => z < 1 :: z is one or minus one
typography/inset.go package: increments = 16 => increments = 17 :: how finely a bevel is moved in decides only which of two steps vanishing within one share goes first
typography/inset.go insetRing: step := 1 => step := 2 :: how finely a bevel is moved in decides only which of two steps vanishing within one share goes first
typography/inset.go inset: if err != nil { return solid{}, nil, err } => if err != nil { } :: a ring that cannot be moved in is nil, and kept refuses a nil outer or hole as having no area
typography/inset.go vanish: if len(alive) < 3 { return nil, nil, errBevel } => if len(alive) < 3 { } :: with fewer than three steps left their lines meet themselves, which meet nowhere: errBevel either way
typography/inset.go vanish: worst, back := -1, 0.0 => worst, back := -2, 0.0 :: worst is asked only whether it is below nought
typography/inset.go kept: !(hole.area() < 0) || !one.outer.holds(hole[0]) => !(hole.area() < 0) || !one.outer.holds(hole[1]) :: rings that do not cross, which crossed has made sure of, hold every point of a ring inside them or none
manufacture/kinds.go made: sprays := 0 => sprays := 1 :: sprays is read only when the tree has branchlets, and then it is drawn; a spruce has none, so its count is never read
# maths, in from its road at d4b2e4b: its own lines, under maths/
maths/space.go package: dimensions = 3 // defined: space has three axes => dimensions = 4 // defined: space has three axes :: dimensions is used once, as the length of the axis array in Hit, which lists three; a fourth element is the zero struct, whose dir is nought and whose at lies inside lo and hi (all nought), so it takes the continue and changes nothing
maths/space.go Hit: if !(enter <= leave) { return nought, nought, false } => if !(enter <= leave) { } :: the final guard refuses the same inputs a step later. enter only grows and leave only shrinks, so enter past leave stays so, and a no-number is cleared only by an infinity that makes enter plus infinity or leave minus infinity, both refused at the end. Twenty million random rays and boxes drawn from zeros, signs, infinities, no-numbers and extremes gave the same answer to the bit with and without the return
# ninjafilm, in from its road at no-lamb 6b95055: its own lines, under
# ninjafilm/
ninjafilm/span/span.go package: inclusive = 1 // defined: both ends of a span are in it => inclusive = 2 // defined: both ends of a span are in it :: makes the scan of the observer's wavelengths run to 831 as well; libtheme's Monochrome is no light there, which scores nought against any hue and cannot beat the positive score a lamp's own wavelength has, so the three wavelengths are the same
ninjafilm/span/span.go each: all := make([]int, stretch.to-stretch.from+inclusive) => all := make([]int, stretch.to-stretch.from-inclusive) :: scans 360 to 828 and leaves out 829 and 830; the lamps' dominant wavelengths are 611, 549 and 464, so no lamp's wavelength moves and no test can see the missing two
ninjafilm/span/span.go each: all := make([]int, stretch.to-stretch.from+inclusive) => all := make([]int, stretch.to+stretch.from+inclusive) :: scans 360 to 1550, the 720 nanometres past 830 being no light in libtheme's Monochrome, which scores nought against any hue and cannot beat the positive score a lamp's own wavelength has, so the three wavelengths are the same
ninjafilm/lens/expose.go package: onePixel = 1 // defined: a frame of one pixel across and one down => onePixel = 2 // defined: a frame of one pixel across and one down :: frame.New gives every pixel no light, no cover and an endless depth whatever the size of the frame, so the first pixel of a frame of two by two is the pixel of a frame of one by one
ninjafilm/lens/lens.go concentric: math.Abs(abscissa) > math.Abs(ordinate) => math.Abs(abscissa) >= math.Abs(ordinate) :: where the two are equal in size both branches of the concentric map give the same point on the aperture, one reached by the radius abscissa and the other by the radius ordinate, so only rounding in the last bit could tell them apart
ninjafilm/lens/lens.go through: abscissa == none && ordinate == none => abscissa == none || ordinate == none :: the r2 sequence puts either coordinate at exactly the middle of its side only at index nought, where both are there, since the other indices land on irrational places; so no pixel has one and not the other
ninjafilm/maths/maths.go package: upward = 1 // defined: math.IsInf's code for infinity above all numbers => upward = 2 // defined: math.IsInf's code for infinity above all numbers :: math.IsInf takes any positive sign to mean infinity above all numbers, so two asks the same question as one
# biome: changes at an edge no noise or hash lands on, a guard no tile
# reaches, and a cap past what the colours are mixed by.
biome/biome.go Make: wet(t) || height[t] < 0.12 || f.Wet(f.Tiling.Centre(t)) < 0.1 || chance(t, 1) > 0.03 => wet(t) || height[t] <= 0.12 || f.Wet(f.Tiling.Centre(t)) < 0.1 || chance(t, 1) > 0.03 :: a tile's height is fractal noise less the sea, and it is 0.12 exactly only by a chance of nought; biome promises rivers from high ground, not which side of 0.12 they start
biome/biome.go Make: wet(t) || height[t] < 0.12 || f.Wet(f.Tiling.Centre(t)) < 0.1 || chance(t, 1) > 0.03 => wet(t) || height[t] < 0.12 || f.Wet(f.Tiling.Centre(t)) <= 0.1 || chance(t, 1) > 0.03 :: the wet is fractal noise, 0.1 exactly only by a chance of nought; biome promises rivers from wet ground, not which side of 0.1
biome/biome.go Make: wet(t) || height[t] < 0.12 || f.Wet(f.Tiling.Centre(t)) < 0.1 || chance(t, 1) > 0.03 => wet(t) || height[t] < 0.12 || f.Wet(f.Tiling.Centre(t)) < 0.1 || chance(t, 1) >= 0.03 :: a hash is 0.03 exactly only by a chance of nought; three in a hundred is a share, not a promise of the edge
biome/biome.go Make: 400 => 401 :: a river is cut after 400 tiles as a guard; each step is strictly lower, so a river is never longer than its map, and no map the tests or the scenes make holds one 400 tiles long
biome/biome.go Make: height[n] < low => height[n] <= low :: two neighbours of fractal noise are as high as each other only by a chance of nought, so the lowest is the same either way
biome/biome.go Make: height[t] < 0.26 || chance(t, 2) > 0.4 => height[t] <= 0.26 || chance(t, 2) > 0.4 :: a tile is 0.26 above the sea exactly only by a chance of nought; biome promises volcanoes on high peaks, not which side of 0.26
biome/biome.go Make: height[t] < 0.26 || chance(t, 2) > 0.4 => height[t] < 0.26 || chance(t, 2) >= 0.4 :: a hash is 0.4 exactly only by a chance of nought; it is a share of peaks, not a promise of the edge
biome/biome.go Make: height[n] >= height[t] => height[n] > height[t] :: a neighbour of fractal noise is exactly as high as a peak only by a chance of nought, so higher than everything round it is the same either way
biome/biome.go Make: wet(t) || w.river[t] || b == snow || b == rock || b == ash || chance(t, 3) > 0.18 => wet(t) || w.river[t] || b == snow || b == rock || b == ash || chance(t, 3) >= 0.18 :: a hash is 0.18 exactly only by a chance of nought; one in a few is a share, not a promise of the edge
biome/biome.go Make: chance(t, 8) < 1.0/30 => chance(t, 8) <= 1.0/30 :: a hash is a thirtieth exactly only by a chance of nought; one heart in thirty cities is a share, not a promise of the edge
biome/biome.go sprawl: step <= reach && len(ring) > 0 => step <= reach && len(ring) >= 0 :: an empty ring lights nothing and finds no neighbours, so going on round with it only counts the steps out to the reach
biome/biome.go armsOf: reference := maths.V(0, 1, 0) => reference := maths.V(0, 2, 0) :: the reference is only taken off the way out and made one long, which a reference twice as long gives alike; where twice its length moves the switch, the arms are measured from another way across the ground, which turns them as the hash's turn does
biome/biome.go armsOf: if math.Abs(out.Dot(reference)) > 0.9 { reference = maths.V(1, 0, 0) } => if math.Abs(out.Dot(reference)) > 0.9 { } :: the switch guards a heart whose way out is up itself, where up less the way out is nothing; no tile of a sphere, band or flat map has its middle on the y axis, and off it the frame is level either way, only turned
biome/biome.go armsOf: math.Abs(out.Dot(reference)) > 0.9 => math.Abs(out.Dot(reference)) >= 0.9 :: a way out is 0.9 up exactly only by a chance of nought, and either reference gives a level frame there, only turned
biome/biome.go armsOf: math.Abs(out.Dot(reference)) > 0.9 => math.Abs(out.Dot(reference)) > 2.8 :: as with the switch taken out: no tile's middle is on the y axis, so the frame is level without it, only turned
biome/biome.go armsOf: reference = maths.V(1, 0, 0) => reference = maths.V(2, 0, 0) :: the reference is only taken off the way out and made one long, which a reference twice as long gives alike
biome/biome.go armsOf: reference = maths.V(1, 0, 0) => reference = maths.V(1, 1, 0) :: taken only near the poles, any reference off the way out gives a level frame, only turned, as the hash's turn turns the arms
biome/biome.go armsOf: reference = maths.V(1, 0, 0) => reference = maths.V(1, 0, 1) :: taken only near the poles, any reference off the way out gives a level frame, only turned, as the hash's turn turns the arms
biome/biome.go sprawled: colour := along(g.w.Lights, math.Min(level, 1)) => colour := along(g.w.Lights, math.Min(level, 2)) :: along gives the last colour for every share from one up, so a share cut at two is the colour a share cut at one is
# observer: a guard whose fall-through gives what it returns.
observer/observer.go FalseColour: if !ok { return swatch.Black } => if !ok { } :: celestial's Onto gives nought for a wavelength it does not map, and libtheme's monochrome of nought nanometres is black, so falling through gives the black the guard returns; it is kept so the colour does not rest on what an unmapped answer happens to be
# sets: the moment a set is drawn at, strconv, yaml's blocks, a keyed
# run's guards, carried with it from interfaces/css, and a black body's
# light.
cmd/house/draw.go package: still = 0 => still = 1 :: a set's world holds still things and lamps with no pulse; the one thing that moves is a body turning on its day, and a nanosecond of saturn's turns it under a millionth of a degree, far below any pixel house draws
cmd/house/draw.go Set: value, err := strconv.ParseFloat(strings.TrimSpace(part), 64) => value, err := strconv.ParseFloat(strings.TrimSpace(part), 65) :: strconv reads a bit size of anything but 32 as 64 (parseFloatPrefix tests bitSize == 32 alone)
set/pieces.go valueNode: i+1 < len(block.Content) => i+1 <= len(block.Content) :: valueNode is only given a block the set has already read as keys, a mapping, whose content is keys and values in pairs; i runs over the even indices, so i+1 never equals the length and the two tests stop at the same place
stage/stage.go package: still = 0 => still = 1 :: the lamps stage lights are bulbs with no pulse, and a bulb's light at any moment is its light; nothing else stage builds asks the moment
scene/thing/keyed.go At: if x < 0 { x = 0 } => if x < 0 { } :: a share below nought is eased to what nought is by every ease but linear, since a curve is never below nought and the halvings take the same half at every step; for linear and a name that is no ease it is at or before the first frame, whose share is nought or more by Keyframe's doc and the set's percent from 0 to 100; TestEasesBelowNoughtAndPastOne holds the numbers
scene/thing/keyed.go At: x < 0 => x <= 0 :: at a share of exactly nought the change sets nought to nought
scene/thing/keyed.go At: x = 1 => x = 2 :: past the end of a held run, 1 and 2 are eased to the same share by every ease but ease-in, where 1 is eased to the float below one and 2 to one, so the place held is the last frame less one part in 2^53 of the move against the last frame itself; a test that asked for the last frame exactly would fail the code as it is, and TestEasesBelowNoughtAndPastOne holds the numbers
stage/stage.go blackbody: math.IsNaN(scale) || math.IsInf(scale, 0) => math.IsNaN(scale) || math.IsInf(scale, 1) :: the luminance asked is nought or more, the set refuses less, and a black body's luminance under the 1931 observer is never below nought, so the scale is never infinity below all numbers; nought over nought is NaN and anything over nought is infinity above

# Finite-light series and the closed form agree within the 1e-6 accuracy
# contract held by independent observer-centred ray-chord integration.
emit/volume.go volumeFalloff: q <= 0.25 => q < 0.25 :: both forms agree at q=0.25; the near-series relative remainder is below 1.1e-8
emit/volume.go volumeFalloff: q >= 4 => q > 4 :: both forms agree at q=4 within 5e-7 relative, below the 1e-6 integral accuracy contract
emit/volume.go volumeFalloff: q >= 4 => q >= 5 :: on 4<=q<5 the closed form replaces a series whose relative remainder is below 5e-7, improving accuracy within the 1e-6 contract
emit/volume.go NewVolume: !(size > 0) || math.IsInf(size, 1) => !(size > 0) || math.IsInf(size, 2) :: math.IsInf treats every positive sign as positive infinity; 1 and 2 ask the same question

