scene/shape/group.go split: if axis == 1 { return point.Y } => if axis == 1 { } :: this still permutes every aligned member/box pair, splits into nonempty ranges and unions the same bounds; hierarchy layout and member order are unspecified, and the GPU rebuilds its own tree; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt records the limits
scene/shape/group.go split: axis == 1 => axis != 1 :: this still permutes every aligned member/box pair, splits into nonempty ranges and unions the same bounds; hierarchy layout and member order are unspecified, and the GPU rebuilds its own tree; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt records the limits
scene/shape/group.go split: axis == 1 => axis == 2 :: this still permutes every aligned member/box pair, splits into nonempty ranges and unions the same bounds; hierarchy layout and member order are unspecified, and the GPU rebuilds its own tree; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt records the limits
scene/shape/group.go split: return centre(group.boxes[order[i]]) < centre(group.boxes[order[j]]) => return centre(group.boxes[order[i]]) <= centre(group.boxes[order[j]]) :: this still permutes every aligned member/box pair, splits into nonempty ranges and unions the same bounds; hierarchy layout and member order are unspecified, and the GPU rebuilds its own tree; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt records the limits
scene/shape/group.go split: return centre(group.boxes[order[i]]) < centre(group.boxes[order[j]]) => return centre(group.boxes[order[i]]) >= centre(group.boxes[order[j]]) :: this still permutes every aligned member/box pair, splits into nonempty ranges and unions the same bounds; hierarchy layout and member order are unspecified, and the GPU rebuilds its own tree; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt records the limits
scene/shape/group.go split: middle := first + (last-first)/2 => middle := first + (last-first)/3 :: this still permutes every aligned member/box pair, splits into nonempty ranges and unions the same bounds; hierarchy layout and member order are unspecified, and the GPU rebuilds its own tree; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt records the limits
scene/shape/group.go hit: if _, _, hit := node.box.Hit(ray, near, far); !hit { continue } => if _, _, hit := node.box.Hit(ray, near, far); !hit { } :: removing box pruning visits more candidates with the same exact ray/window tests and nearest answer; this changes traversal cost, not promised geometry; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt
scene/shape/group.go nearest: if groupBoxDistance(node.box, point) > best { continue } => if groupBoxDistance(node.box, point) > best { } :: removing a lower-bound pruning branch visits more surfaces but takes the same nearest answer; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt records geometry versus traversal cost
scene/shape/group.go nearest: groupBoxDistance(node.box, point) > best => groupBoxDistance(node.box, point) >= best :: only equally near member surfaces change selection; exact ties do not prescribe a component or its normal/sign; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt
scene/shape/group.go nearest: math.Abs(distance) < best => math.Abs(distance) <= best :: only equally near member surfaces change selection; exact ties do not prescribe a component or its normal/sign; scene/shape/testdata/group-hierarchy-equivalence.txt
fuelbed/bed.go At: target < b.tick => target <= b.tick :: at the current completed tick, resetting and replaying the same immutable initial cells gives the identical owned snapshot; this adds CPU work but changes no state or returned value
fuelbed/bed.go ticks: return 0, fmt.Errorf("fuelbed: time must be finite within 0..86400s") => return 1, fmt.Errorf("fuelbed: time must be finite within 0..86400s") :: both callers discard the tick count when err is nonnil and return that error; the unused numeric error result has no promise
fuelbed/metal.go newDevice: d := &Device{session: session, spec: s, params: make([]byte, 32)} => d := &Device{session: session, spec: s, params: make([]byte, 33)} :: the embedded Metal stencil reads the same 32 packed parameter bytes; a trailing zero byte has no reader and changes no binding or result
fuelbed/metal.go At: end := min(target, d.tick+128) => end := min(target, d.tick+129) :: only the commit partition changes from 128 to 129 ticks; the same ordered ping-pong dispatches run to the same target and produce identical snapshots, with no promised batch-size API

scene/medium/grove.go NewGrove: !grove.indexable(lo.X) || !grove.indexable(hi.X) || !grove.indexable(lo.Z) || !grove.indexable(hi.Z) => !grove.indexable(lo.X) && !grove.indexable(hi.X) || !grove.indexable(lo.Z) || !grove.indexable(hi.Z) :: on the compiled 64-bit canopy domain cell>=radius makes both endpoint coordinates round together near the host index limit; scene/medium/testdata/grove-endpoint-index-domain.txt proves it; the 32-bit-specific canopy regression guards the non-equivalent case there

gpu/bake.go bakeWisps: cells := make([]byte, 0, encoded.cells*4) => cells := make([]byte, 0, encoded.cells/4) :: this changes only the initial capacity of a zero-length host byte slice; the same float words are appended in the same order and only the resulting length/data are bound or reported, so reallocations and host memory cost can differ but rendered data and GPU work do not
gpu/bake.go bakeWisps: cells := make([]byte, 0, encoded.cells*4) => cells := make([]byte, 0, encoded.cells*5) :: this changes only the initial capacity of a zero-length host byte slice; the same float words are appended in the same order and only the resulting length/data are bound or reported, so reallocations and host memory cost can differ but rendered data and GPU work do not

# Renderer metadata and failed-deferral equivalences.
gpu/flame_bake.go flameParameters: composite = 1 => composite = 2 :: composite is serialized only into FireGrid.seed.w; fire_grid reads that word only with == 0 to choose single-source or composite emission, so both nonzero flag values select the identical branch and all samples, dimensions and reported byte counts remain unchanged
gpu/flame_bake.go flameParameters: []float64{light.R, light.G, light.B, 0} => []float64{light.R, light.G, light.B, 1} :: the fourth float is alignment padding in each float4 thermal-table row; every table access in fire_glow reads only xyz, so changing w leaves all three emission channels, row strides and reported byte counts unchanged
gpu/flame_grid.go flamePieces: return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 => return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 || len(pieces) <= 64 :: flameField is the sole production caller; public Field values have at most 64 pieces because NewField bounds construction and Pieces only filters, and an empty density list is still rejected by the unchanged matching-count and nonempty-emission checks before any bake is reserved
gpu/flame_grid.go flamePieces: return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 => return pieces, true, len(pieces) >= 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 :: this only admits the empty density list from an inactive or zero Field; flameField is the sole production caller and still rejects it through the unchanged matching-count and nonempty-emission checks before reserving a bake
gpu/flame_grid.go flamePieces: return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 => return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 65 :: NewField accepts at most 64 sources and Pieces only filters them, so a supported immutable Field cannot reach the added 65-source case; flameField also requires equal counts with the unchanged emission recognizer, which still rejects counts above 64
gpu/flame_grid.go glowingPieces: return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 => return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 || len(pieces) <= 64 :: flameField is the sole production caller and first requires a nonempty density list of at most 64 pieces; its unchanged equal-count check still rejects every emission list outside that range, so broadening this second range check cannot reserve a different bake
gpu/flame_grid.go glowingPieces: return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 => return pieces, true, len(pieces) >= 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 :: only the empty emission list is newly accepted by this helper; its sole caller flameField already has a nonempty density list and still requires equal source counts before reserving a bake, so the empty emission case remains refused
gpu/flame_grid.go glowingPieces: return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 64 => return pieces, true, len(pieces) > 0 && len(pieces) <= 65 :: public Field construction is capped at 64 and Pieces only filters; the sole caller flameField also requires equal source counts with its unchanged density recognizer, which refuses counts above 64, so allowing 65 here cannot reserve a different bake
gpu/flame_grid.go deferFlame: return -1, -1, false => return +1, -1, false :: false selects CPU sampling in boxedVolume, the sole production caller; it overwrites both returned offsets with the actual appended density and shine ranges before packing them, so the failed-deferral placeholder is never read or bound
gpu/flame_grid.go deferFlame: return -1, -1, false => return -2, -1, false :: boxedVolume is the sole production caller and overwrites both offsets after false selects CPU sampling, before packing any grid metadata; changing the unused failed-deferral density placeholder cannot change a buffer or rendered sample
gpu/flame_grid.go deferFlame: return -1, -1, false => return -1, +1, false :: false selects CPU sampling in the sole production caller boxedVolume; it replaces both density and emission offsets with the appended CPU grid ranges before packing them, so this failed-deferral emission placeholder is unused
gpu/flame_grid.go deferFlame: return -1, -1, false => return -1, -2, false :: boxedVolume overwrites both returned offsets with the appended CPU density and emission ranges whenever deferral is false, before packing any metadata; this unused failed-deferral emission placeholder cannot change a buffer or sample

gpu/instances.go package: topLeaf = 2 => topLeaf = 3 :: this changes an unspecified hierarchy partition and its storage/traversal cost, not the represented instances; splitTops still covers every member in nonempty ranges, both packing and the native plan use the same topped order, and both shader walks iterate the actual leaf count; the larger leaves only reduce depth/node count under the existing buffer/stack bounds, while exact coincident-hit tie selection is not specified
gpu/lighting_bake.go bakeLighting: binary.LittleEndian.PutUint32(encoded.header.bytes[offset:], 0) => binary.LittleEndian.PutUint32(encoded.header.bytes[offset:], 1) :: this private header selector is read only by smoke_light and is overwritten with each job index immediately before dispatch; trace never reads it, so its idle value after the last bake changes neither lighting nor tracing, including a later bake that again overwrites it before use

gpu/native.go accelerationPlan: return nil, 0, err => return nil, 1, err :: this integer is a native root index, not a duration; both error branches retain the nil plan and the original error, and sampledLightingGrid, the sole production caller, skips Accelerate and returns that error without reading root; the unused error placeholder has no promised value
gpu/native.go populateAcceleration: return 0, err => return 1, err :: this integer is the native root and has no promised value when err is nonnil; accelerationPlan, the sole production caller, checks that error and returns a nil plan without reading the root, and sampledLightingGrid then skips Accelerate; all branches represented by this repeated return key preserve the same original error
gpu/native.go populateAcceleration: return 0, fmt.Errorf("gpu: invalid shared group %d", standing.group) => return 1, fmt.Errorf("gpu: invalid shared group %d", standing.group) :: the original invalid-group error is unchanged, and accelerationPlan checks that error before using the returned native root; sampledLightingGrid then returns the error without calling Accelerate, so this private unused error-result integer has no promised value
gpu/native.go triangleRange: return start, max(first+count, other+more) - start => return start, max(first-count, other+more) - start :: grow.split partitions every nonempty mesh range into contiguous [first,middle) and [middle,last) children, and pool adds the same face offset to both; the right endpoint is therefore always later than the left endpoint, and replacing the unused smaller max operand first+count with the still-smaller first-count cannot change the returned interval; empty roots return before this expression and all indices remain within allocated slice lengths
gpu/native.go nativeFirsts: first := 0 => first := 1 :: both arms of the immediately following group test assign first unconditionally, either from firstOf(standing.root) or from firstMember[standing.group-1], before packed.word reads it; this overwritten initializer never reaches a buffer or any caller
gpu/parts.go treeWithin: bend := -1 => bend := -2 :: for flat faces both software and native mesh-hit shader paths test the packed bend index only for being nonnegative before reading normals, so either exactly representable negative value selects the same geometric normal; native acceleration reads only each vertex's xyz and ignores this w lane, and smooth faces overwrite bend with their actual nonnegative index before packing
gpu/parts.go package: distantBeyond = -1 => distantBeyond = -2 :: lamp_light is the only shader reader of the packed beyond value and returns the same fixed distant-light direction, unbounded reach and strength for every negative value before any point-light distance arithmetic; lampsOf uses this private marker only for emit.Distant, so either exactly representable negative value has the same rendering and profiling behavior
gpu/parts.go camera: encoded.header.row(headerStruct, values{ "origin": point(eye.Out(maths.Vec{})), "right": point(axes.Out(maths.XAxis)), "up": point(axes.Out(maths.YAxis)), "back": point(axes.Out(maths.ZAxis)), "lens": {math.Tan(wide / 2), aspect, float64(width), float64(height)}, "counts": wholes(counts.spheres, counts.triangles, counts.nodes, counts.lamps), "more": wholes(counts.volumes, strides, shades, counts.trees), "tuning": {lift, faint, evenly, encoded.spread}, "tops": wholes(counts.instances, counts.tops), "look": wholes(flag(encoded.looked)), "lighting": wholes(0)}) => encoded.header.row(headerStruct, values{ "origin": point(eye.Out(maths.Vec{})), "right": point(axes.Out(maths.XAxis)), "up": point(axes.Out(maths.YAxis)), "back": point(axes.Out(maths.ZAxis)), "lens": {math.Tan(wide / 2), aspect, float64(width), float64(height)}, "counts": wholes(counts.spheres, counts.triangles, counts.nodes, counts.lamps), "more": wholes(counts.volumes, strides, shades, counts.trees), "tuning": {lift, faint, evenly, encoded.spread}, "tops": wholes(counts.instances, counts.tops), "look": wholes(flag(encoded.looked)), "lighting": wholes(1)}) :: the private lighting selector is read only by smoke_light; tracing ignores it, and bakeLighting writes each actual volume index immediately before every smoke_light dispatch, so the initial header value is never consumed by a production render
gpu/profile.go nativeCounts: index < len(samples) => index <= len(samples) :: sampledLightingGrid is the sole production caller and passes a successful Grid result with stride perSample, which is 12, 23 or 34; Grid returns empty or exactly width*height*stride floats, while this loop visits indices 11+k*stride and therefore never equals that length; Grid also bounds the allocation by MaxInt/4, so the final increment cannot wrap; partial private arrays are not a renderer result
gpu/sampled.go package: rows = 64 => rows = 65 :: this private scheduling choice changes band staging storage and dispatch cost, not the rendered sample/cell coordinates; Grid clamps bands to height, draw_bands covers each row once and copies to its global row offset, and trace, smoke_light and the bake kernels add the supplied band origin before evaluating their data, so partitioning supported work into 65-row rather than 64-row submissions preserves frame and grid results within the same device resource domain; no public API promises this exact batch height or identical dispatch timing
gpu/scene.go package: rayWords = 6 => rayWords = 7 :: rayWords is used only for the initial capacity of a zero-length private ray buffer; ray still appends exactly two xyz vectors per pixel, shader indexing remains six floats, and Grid and Profile use the resulting slice length rather than capacity, so this changes reservation cost but not encoded rays or rendered results within supported allocation bounds
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(-1, -1) => given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(+1, -1) :: volume writes only puffKind or wispyKind records; both shader reads of shone offsets are inside the boxed_kind and boxed_part guard, and boxedVolume has its own unchanged writer, so these private unused puff emission/paint offsets cannot change a sampled value or render
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(-1, -1) => given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(-2, -1) :: volume writes only puffKind or wispyKind records; both shader reads of shone offsets are inside the boxed_kind and boxed_part guard, and boxedVolume has its own unchanged writer, so these private unused puff emission/paint offsets cannot change a sampled value or render
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(-1, -1) => given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(-1, +1) :: volume writes only puffKind or wispyKind records; both shader reads of shone offsets are inside the boxed_kind and boxed_part guard, and boxedVolume has its own unchanged writer, so these private unused puff emission/paint offsets cannot change a sampled value or render
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(-1, -1) => given["grid"], given["shone"] = nil, wholes(-1, -2) :: volume writes only puffKind or wispyKind records; both shader reads of shone offsets are inside the boxed_kind and boxed_part guard, and boxedVolume has its own unchanged writer, so these private unused puff emission/paint offsets cannot change a sampled value or render
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(1, 0, 0, -1) :: a negative lighting.w offset makes scattered return direct lighting before reading any grid dimension or data; both negative markers select that branch, and puff or wispy records are never queued for smoke_light, so these inactive dimensions or negative marker variants do not change rendering or preparation
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 1, 0, -1) :: a negative lighting.w offset makes scattered return direct lighting before reading any grid dimension or data; both negative markers select that branch, and puff or wispy records are never queued for smoke_light, so these inactive dimensions or negative marker variants do not change rendering or preparation
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 1, -1) :: a negative lighting.w offset makes scattered return direct lighting before reading any grid dimension or data; both negative markers select that branch, and puff or wispy records are never queued for smoke_light, so these inactive dimensions or negative marker variants do not change rendering or preparation
gpu/volumes.go volume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -2) :: a negative lighting.w offset makes scattered return direct lighting before reading any grid dimension or data; both negative markers select that branch, and puff or wispy records are never queued for smoke_light, so these inactive dimensions or negative marker variants do not change rendering or preparation
gpu/volumes.go boxedVolume: painted := -1 => painted := -2 :: the CPU-sampled branch overwrites painted with the actual paint range or its no-data result; the deferred branch has no paint texture and shader scattering tests the retained paint offset only for being nonnegative, so either negative marker preserves its material colour while a positive marker is not equivalent
gpu/volumes.go boxedVolume: given["info"] = wholes(0, 0, boxedKind, material) => given["info"] = wholes(1, 0, boxedKind, material) :: these words are procedural-wisp seed lanes; boxed density takes the sampled-grid branch before live, and the wisp baker queues only wispy puff records, while boxedVolume retains boxedKind and its already sampled CPU/deferred flame data, so these unused packed seeds do not change the medium or render
gpu/volumes.go boxedVolume: given["info"] = wholes(0, 0, boxedKind, material) => given["info"] = wholes(0, 1, boxedKind, material) :: these words are procedural-wisp seed lanes; boxed density takes the sampled-grid branch before live, and the wisp baker queues only wispy puff records, while boxedVolume retains boxedKind and its already sampled CPU/deferred flame data, so these unused packed seeds do not change the medium or render
gpu/volumes.go boxedVolume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(1, 0, 0, -1) :: direct scattering returns before dimensions or grid data whenever lighting.w is negative; both negative markers select that path, while for cacheable smoke reserveLightingAt overwrites all four lighting lanes before any smoke_light dispatch or tracing, so these inactive initial dimensions or negative-marker variants cannot change sampled lighting
gpu/volumes.go boxedVolume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 1, 0, -1) :: direct scattering returns before dimensions or grid data whenever lighting.w is negative; both negative markers select that path, while for cacheable smoke reserveLightingAt overwrites all four lighting lanes before any smoke_light dispatch or tracing, so these inactive initial dimensions or negative-marker variants cannot change sampled lighting
gpu/volumes.go boxedVolume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 1, -1) :: direct scattering returns before dimensions or grid data whenever lighting.w is negative; both negative markers select that path, while for cacheable smoke reserveLightingAt overwrites all four lighting lanes before any smoke_light dispatch or tracing, so these inactive initial dimensions or negative-marker variants cannot change sampled lighting
gpu/volumes.go boxedVolume: given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -1) => given["lighting"] = wholes(0, 0, 0, -2) :: direct scattering returns before dimensions or grid data whenever lighting.w is negative; both negative markers select that path, while for cacheable smoke reserveLightingAt overwrites all four lighting lanes before any smoke_light dispatch or tracing, so these inactive initial dimensions or negative-marker variants cannot change sampled lighting

# hillside: observed source and numerical equivalences; every case still runs.
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go prepareFronts: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: the checked plume changes only its finite positive base; its other validity conditions are retained; checked source changes only seed and finite wind; a bad private exposure reaches the same later palette refusal before retained state changes; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go prepareFronts: start, end := events[0].Start, events[0].Start+events[0].Duration => start, end := events[0].Start, events[0].Start-events[0].Duration :: the first nonempty positive-duration event restores the normal end before its first read; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: if at < group.start || at >= group.end { continue } => if at < group.start || at >= group.end { } :: the immutable field pieces enforce their own activity windows; removing this prefilter adds work but no scene contribution; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: at < group.start || at >= group.end => at < group.start && at >= group.end :: the immutable field pieces enforce their own activity windows; removing this prefilter adds work but no scene contribution; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: at < group.start || at >= group.end => at < group.start || at > group.end :: the immutable field pieces enforce their own activity windows; removing this prefilter adds work but no scene contribution; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: len(active) > 0 && front.smoke != nil => len(active) >= 0 && front.smoke != nil :: an empty list leaves zero weight and the following positive-weight guard suppresses all construction and append; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: spec.Yield *= math.Min(1, weight/float64(front.peakFuel)) => spec.Yield *= math.Min(2, weight/float64(front.peakFuel)) :: normalized occupancy is at most one; documented rounding bounds cap differences below 1.2e-11 relative yield, not bit identity; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: if at < event.start || age >= event.duration+event.sparks.life { continue } => if at < event.start || age >= event.duration+event.sparks.life { } :: the emitter lifetime and adapter burn-birth filters reject these particles independently; only redundant work changes; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: at < event.start || age >= event.duration+event.sparks.life => at < event.start && age >= event.duration+event.sparks.life :: the emitter lifetime and adapter burn-birth filters reject these particles independently; only redundant work changes; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_front.go addAt: at < event.start || age >= event.duration+event.sparks.life => at < event.start || age > event.duration+event.sparks.life :: the emitter lifetime and adapter burn-birth filters reject these particles independently; only redundant work changes; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_recipe.go putFireRecipe: if err != nil { return maths.Box{}, err } => if err != nil { } :: the reader checked the copied factory/source and exposure; the sampled type is fixed, and the one-nanosecond light slice is discarded; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_recipe.go putFireRecipe: volumes, _, err := made.Place(at, stage.given.exposure, 0) => volumes, _, err := made.Place(at, stage.given.exposure, 1) :: the reader checked the copied factory/source and exposure; the sampled type is fixed, and the one-nanosecond light slice is discarded; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_recipe.go putFireRecipe: if !ok { return maths.Box{}, fmt.Errorf("fire has no sampled emission") } => if !ok { } :: the reader checked the copied factory/source and exposure; the sampled type is fixed, and the one-nanosecond light slice is discarded; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_sparks.go prepareSparks: if err != nil { return nil, err } => if err != nil { } :: the shared octahedron is fixed and valid; private burn durations are zero or at least one millisecond; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fire_sparks.go thingsIn: duration > 0 && at-particle.Age() >= duration => duration > 1 && at-particle.Age() >= duration :: the shared octahedron is fixed and valid; private burn durations are zero or at least one millisecond; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fuel_bed.go prepareBeds: group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} => group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0-float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} :: private origin cancels in world placement; 30 representative Metal cases pass the existing 1e-4 composite budget; no universal scene or image bound is claimed; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fuel_bed.go prepareBeds: group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} => group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)/cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} :: private origin cancels in world placement; 30 representative Metal cases pass the existing 1e-4 composite budget; no universal scene or image bound is claimed; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fuel_bed.go prepareBeds: group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} => group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 1, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} :: private origin cancels in world placement; 30 representative Metal cases pass the existing 1e-4 composite budget; no universal scene or image bound is claimed; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fuel_bed.go prepareBeds: group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} => group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0-float64(gz)*cell)} :: private origin cancels in world placement; 30 representative Metal cases pass the existing 1e-4 composite budget; no universal scene or image bound is claimed; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fuel_bed.go prepareBeds: group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)*cell)} => group := fuelGroup{anchor: maths.V(x0+float64(gx)*cell, 0, z0+float64(gz)/cell)} :: private origin cancels in world placement; 30 representative Metal cases pass the existing 1e-4 composite budget; no universal scene or image bound is claimed; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/fuel_bed.go prepareBeds: at := maths.V(x0+(float64(x)+.5)*cell, 0, z0+(float64(z)+.5)*cell) => at := maths.V(x0+(float64(x)+.5)*cell, 1, z0+(float64(z)+.5)*cell) :: ground height and clearance overwrite this temporary Y before any read; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md
cmd/hillside/scene.go moonlit: if err != nil { return way, err } => if err != nil { } :: the globe constructor receives only the fixed positive finite lunar mean radius; see doc/hillside-mutation-equivalents.md

# The demo menu is embedded and checked by the real catalogue command test.
# Revisit this judgement if callers can supply or change catalogue bytes.
cmd/demo/show.go show: if catalogueErr != nil { return catalogueErr } => if catalogueErr != nil { } :: the schema and menu are immutable embedded bytes verified by the shipped catalogue test; no caller data or I/O can make this read fail, and the production error check remains

# Sampling budgets are immutable embedded data; public bounds/width tests use
# all three values. These two changes cannot affect the shipped catalogue.
render/beamstep/beamstep.go readChoices: if err != nil { panic(err) } => if err != nil { } :: numbers.Read receives the immutable embedded choices.yaml; the shipped budgets parse successfully, so the error arm has no reachable input
render/beamstep/beamstep.go choice: if err != nil { panic(err) } => if err != nil { } :: every requested name exists in immutable choices.yaml and is exercised by the public refinement fixtures; no caller supplies a lookup name or catalogue
scene/medium/layer.go Density: below <= weatherZero || below >= layer.weather.Depth => below <= weatherZero || below > layer.weather.Depth :: at exactly Depth the validated finite weather profile has Smooth(0,Depth,Depth)=1 and zero density; entering that computation instead of returning zero early changes no result

# Exact spacecraft cases; source arguments in doc/spacecraft-mutation-notes.md.
manufacture/instrument_assembly.go rod: if placementError != nil { assembly.trouble = placementError return } => if placementError != nil { } :: newTube already validated the unchanged run.Len as positive and finite; spaceAlong refuses only that same length
manufacture/spacecraft.go Spacecraft: !finite(bounds.Min.Len() + bounds.Max.Len()) => !finite(bounds.Min.Len() - bounds.Max.Len()) :: finite Len results are at most sqrt(MaxFloat64), so their sum and difference are finite; either non-finite Len makes both forms non-finite
manufacture/spacecraft.go extent: width = math.Max(size.radius, aperture* spacecraftValue("spitzer-panel-width")*spaceHalf) => width = math.Min(size.radius, aperture* spacecraftValue("spitzer-panel-width")*spaceHalf) :: immutable radius proportion one and panel-width proportion two make both operands equal at every accepted aperture
manufacture/spacecraft.go extent: width = math.Max(size.radius, aperture* spacecraftValue("spitzer-panel-width")*spaceHalf) => width = math.Max(size.radius, aperture/ spacecraftValue("spitzer-panel-width")*spaceHalf) :: the immutable housing radius equals the aperture and masks either the equal original panel half-width or the smaller divided half-width
manufacture/spacecraft_data.go spacecraftValue: if spacecraftNumbersError != nil { panic(spacecraftNumbersError) } => if spacecraftNumbersError != nil { } :: numbers.Read receives immutable embedded spacecraft-choices.yaml; the shipped document parses and no public input replaces it
manufacture/spacecraft_data.go spacecraftValue: if err != nil { panic(err) } => if err != nil { } :: every lookup name comes from the shipped templates and exists in immutable spacecraft-choices.yaml; no public input supplies a name
manufacture/spacecraft_webb.go exposed: bus := maths.V(-size.radius*spaceOne, busY, -size.radius*spaceOne) => bus := maths.V(-size.radius/spaceOne, busY, -size.radius*spaceOne) :: spaceOne is exactly one; multiplication and division preserve the same first coordinate
manufacture/spacecraft_webb.go exposed: bus := maths.V(-size.radius*spaceOne, busY, -size.radius*spaceOne) => bus := maths.V(-size.radius*spaceOne, busY, -size.radius/spaceOne) :: spaceOne is exactly one; multiplication and division preserve the same last coordinate
set/spacecraft.go package: scenePositiveInfinity = 1 => scenePositiveInfinity = 2 :: math.Inf uses only the sign; both positive arguments return positive infinity
set/spacecraft.go spacecraftRecipe: if requiredError != nil { return requiredError } => if requiredError != nil { } :: missing kind prevents dispatch to spacecraftRecipe, and missing spacecraft leaves zero parameters rejected by Construction immediately afterward
manufacture/slit_cylinder.go package: arcCorners, arcInnerLow, arcInnerHigh = 4, 2, 3 => arcCorners, arcInnerLow, arcInnerHigh = 5, 2, 3 :: point initializes four corner roles; skin and caps address only indices 0, 1, 2 and 3, so the extra zero-filled array slot is never read or emitted as a vertex
